  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate finds an error in a file you prepared. Senior stakeholders know the history, so your response will model how the team handles being wrong. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, correct it, and check whether anything else was affected$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the error and fix the file quickly$qb$, $qb$Explain why the error happened before making the correction$qb$, $qb$Ask them to correct it because they found it$qb$, $qb$Say the error is minor and does not need attention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker asks you to share a customer file they are not authorized to access. The action may later be reviewed under formal governance. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline and direct them to the approved access process$qb$, $qb$Check why they need it before deciding what to do$qb$, $qb$Share only the parts you think are harmless$qb$, $qb$Send it if they promise not to forward it$qb$, $qb$Share it because they work in the same company$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A difficult week ends successfully because several teammates helped one another. The response should reinforce the contribution and the behavior that made it useful. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize specific contributions and also note what the team learned$qb$, $qb$Thank the team and mention the successful result$qb$, $qb$Arrange a small celebration without discussing the work$qb$, $qb$Move straight to the next target while motivation is high$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the mistakes so the team improves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R7-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You ask a colleague in another time zone to complete a task before your next workday. The colleague must act while you are offline and may face a material judgement call. What is the best message?$qb$, $qb$Give the context, exact action, deadline, and link to what they need$qb$, $qb$State the action and deadline with the relevant file attached$qb$, $qb$Send a short request and offer to explain later$qb$, $qb$Send several messages as you remember more details$qb$, $qb$Ask them to call you if they are unsure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague with a hearing difficulty asks for written actions after meetings. The choice affects today's result and the longer-term development pipeline. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Provide clear written actions as a normal part of the meeting follow-up$qb$, $qb$Send written actions to them after each meeting$qb$, $qb$Ask them to remind you whenever they need notes$qb$, $qb$Suggest that a teammate share personal notes with them$qb$, $qb$Say meetings are mainly verbal and cannot suit everyone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate suggests a better solution after you have already presented your idea. Senior stakeholders know the earlier position, so changing course will be visible. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate their solution fairly and support it if it is better$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the advantages before deciding$qb$, $qb$Defend your idea first because it was already presented$qb$, $qb$Combine both ideas even if one is clearly weaker$qb$, $qb$Reject the new idea to avoid changing direction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to approve a check you did not actually perform. The decision may later face formal review and must remain defensible. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not approve it until the required check has been completed$qb$, $qb$Explain that you can approve it after checking the work$qb$, $qb$Approve it if a trusted colleague says the work is correct$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the deadline is close$qb$, $qb$Approve it now and check later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker quietly fixes a data issue before it reaches the client. The response should reinforce the contribution and strengthen future cross-team cooperation. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically and share the useful prevention lesson with the team$qb$, $qb$Thank them and ask how they spotted the issue$qb$, $qb$Say 'good catch' and continue$qb$, $qb$Focus on who created the data issue$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because fixing issues is part of the job$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R8-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague to review a file while you are offline. The colleague must progress while you are unavailable and may face a decision affecting scope or risk. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$Please review sections 2 and 3 by 2 PM, add comments in the file, and message me if a blocker prevents completion$qb$, $qb$Please review sections 2 and 3 by 2 PM and add comments$qb$, $qb$Please review the attached file today$qb$, $qb$Please take a look when you can$qb$, $qb$Can we discuss the file later?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague cannot attend a regular meeting because of an approved work arrangement. The choice affects immediate results and who develops capability for future assignments. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Make key information and decisions available in a way they can access$qb$, $qb$Send them the meeting notes and invite questions$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate to brief them informally$qb$, $qb$Expect them to find out what they missed$qb$, $qb$Remove them from work discussed in that meeting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You strongly supported a plan, but new information shows another approach is better. Senior stakeholders know the earlier decision, so changing direction will be visible. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the new evidence and support the better approach$qb$, $qb$Review the new information and explain why you are changing your view$qb$, $qb$Continue defending your plan until a manager decides$qb$, $qb$Suggest combining both approaches to avoid admitting a change$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new information$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a small expense has been entered twice in a claim. The information may later be reviewed under formal governance and must remain defensible. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the duplicate before the claim is submitted$qb$, $qb$Tell the person preparing the claim and ask them to correct it$qb$, $qb$Submit it and correct it if finance notices$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the amount is small$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the duplicate was accidental$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate gives you useful feedback that improves your final work. The response should recognize contribution while reinforcing the behaviour that enabled the result. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for the specific feedback and tell them how it improved the result$qb$, $qb$Thank them and show them the improved version$qb$, $qb$Say thanks and submit the work$qb$, $qb$Avoid mentioning the feedback because the final work is yours$qb$, $qb$Take full credit because you made the change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R9-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague will pick up your work tomorrow morning. The colleague must progress while you are unavailable and may face a decision affecting scope or risk. Which handover is most useful?$qb$, $qb$Current status, completed work, open issue, next action, owner, and deadline$qb$, $qb$Current status, open issue, and next action$qb$, $qb$A link to the project folder with a short note$qb$, $qb$A message saying 'please continue from here'$qb$, $qb$No message because the files are shared$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team social activity always happens at a time one colleague cannot attend because of a regular commitment. The choice affects today's outcome and the future capability pipeline. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Discuss alternative times or formats so participation is reasonably accessible$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague which other times could work$qb$, $qb$Keep the usual time but send them photos afterward$qb$, $qb$Tell them attendance is optional$qb$, $qb$Exclude them from future planning because they cannot attend$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out that your proposed solution has a serious gap. Senior stakeholders know the earlier position, so changing course will be visible. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, examine the gap, and change the solution if the concern is valid$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the gap and possible alternatives$qb$, $qb$Defend your solution before considering the concern$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide whose view is correct$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the concern because you prepared the solution$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A friend at work asks you to mark them present at a meeting they did not attend. The action may later be reviewed under formal governance and must remain defensible. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Refuse and keep the attendance record accurate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you cannot change the attendance record$qb$, $qb$Mark them present if they had a good reason for missing it$qb$, $qb$Leave the attendance field blank$qb$, $qb$Mark them present because it has no business impact$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate shares a template that makes your work much easier. The response should reinforce both the contribution and the collaborative behaviour behind it. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically and share how the template helped your work$qb$, $qb$Thank them and ask whether the template can be shared with others$qb$, $qb$Use the template and say thanks later$qb$, $qb$Share the template without mentioning who created it$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because templates are meant to be reused$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-5TO10-R10-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish your work late at night and a teammate will continue in the morning. The colleague must progress while you are unavailable and may face a decision affecting scope or risk. What is the best handover?$qb$, $qb$Leave a concise update with status, open issue, next action, and links to the current files$qb$, $qb$State what is complete and what they should do next$qb$, $qb$Send the file links without an explanation$qb$, $qb$Send several messages describing everything you did$qb$, $qb$Wait until morning to explain it live$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A useful assignment is always given to people who have done it before, so newer colleagues never gain experience. The choice affects current delivery, succession depth, and perceptions of fairness across the function. What is the best improvement?$qb$, $qb$Give suitable newer colleagues a chance with clear support and fair selection criteria$qb$, $qb$Pair a newer colleague with an experienced person on the next assignment$qb$, $qb$Keep using experienced people because the task is important$qb$, $qb$Let experienced people choose who may join them$qb$, $qb$Wait until newer colleagues have more experience elsewhere$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You recommend an approach that later proves slower than a teammate's suggestion. The original decision was visible to senior leadership, so your response will signal the standard for accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the result, adopt the better approach, and share what you learned$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate their approach worked better and use it next time$qb$, $qb$Explain why your approach seemed reasonable at the time$qb$, $qb$Keep your approach for some tasks to justify the earlier choice$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing which approach worked better$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You find that a required check was skipped, although the final result appears correct. The issue has reputational and governance implications and may later face independent scrutiny. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Complete or arrange the required check and record the work accurately$qb$, $qb$Tell the owner the check was missed and ask them to complete it$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the final result looks correct$qb$, $qb$Mark the check complete because no error was found$qb$, $qb$Remove the check from the record$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague takes time to teach you a shortcut that is both safe and useful. The response should recognize contribution while reinforcing the culture and collaboration needed at scale. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, apply it correctly, and acknowledge their help when sharing the improvement$qb$, $qb$Thank them and practice the shortcut on your next task$qb$, $qb$Use the shortcut and mention it only if someone asks$qb$, $qb$Present the shortcut as your own discovery$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because you already have a working method$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R1-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague will review your request after you have logged off. Work must continue across time zones while you are unavailable, including decisions that may affect scope and risk. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$State the decision needed, key context, deadline, and where to find the supporting file$qb$, $qb$State the decision and deadline with the file link$qb$, $qb$Send the file and ask them to review it$qb$, $qb$Write 'urgent—please check'$qb$, $qb$Wait until you are both online$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team member needs captions to follow an online training session. The choice affects immediate delivery, succession depth, and perceived fairness across the function. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use an accessible session or enable accurate captions so they can participate fully$qb$, $qb$Enable available captions and check whether they work for the person$qb$, $qb$Send the recording afterward$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate to summarize the training for them$qb$, $qb$Tell them to attend and read the slides$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You discover that a teammate's suggestion would fix a problem better than the solution you proposed. Senior leadership knows the original position, so your response will set a visible accountability standard. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Support the better solution and openly credit the teammate's contribution$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate you agree and use their suggestion$qb$, $qb$Combine both solutions so your idea remains included$qb$, $qb$Ask a manager which solution should be used$qb$, $qb$Keep your own solution because you proposed it first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to copy a signature from an old document onto a new form to save time. The issue has governance and reputational implications and may later face independent scrutiny. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Refuse and obtain the required approval or signature through the proper process$qb$, $qb$Explain that the new form needs proper authorization$qb$, $qb$Copy it only if the same person would approve the form$qb$, $qb$Ask a coworker whether this is normally done$qb$, $qb$Copy it because the content is routine$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices a risk early and prevents rework for the whole group. The response should recognize contribution while strengthening the culture needed for future cross-functional work. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the specific action and explain the rework it prevented$qb$, $qb$Thank them and tell the team what was avoided$qb$, $qb$Say 'well done' in the team chat$qb$, $qb$Move on because the problem never occurred$qb$, $qb$Focus on why the risk existed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R2-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate in another time zone needs to make a decision before you return online. Work must continue across time zones while you are unavailable, including decisions that may alter scope or risk. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$State the decision needed, options, key facts, deadline, and what they may decide without you$qb$, $qb$State the decision, options, and deadline clearly$qb$, $qb$Send the background file and ask them to decide$qb$, $qb$Write 'please decide urgently'$qb$, $qb$Wait until you are both online$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The choice will affect current delivery, succession depth, and whether development opportunities are seen as credible. How can a team lead best ensure equal growth opportunities for quiet or junior team members during client engagements?$qb$, $qb$Implementing a structured presentation rotation framework supported by peer coaching and preparation sessions.$qb$, $qb$Requesting open volunteers for client meetings while encouraging quieter members to speak up.$qb$, $qb$Assigning major presentation slots directly to quiet members to force immediate public exposure.$qb$, $qb$Consulting with senior management before changing presentation responsibilities for key client accounts.$qb$, $qb$Reserving client-facing presentations for senior staff while assigning internal team updates to junior members.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Senior leaders know you sponsored the original course, and a public change of direction will affect how accountability is modeled. Which behavior demonstrates a true accountability mindset when a newly deployed process update causes minor tracking errors?$qb$, $qb$Owning the mistake transparently with affected stakeholders, implementing an immediate fix, and documenting lessons learned.$qb$, $qb$Apologizing to affected teams and offering to process their daily reports manually until errors subside.$qb$, $qb$Explaining to stakeholders that initial system errors are standard when deploying complex operational updates.$qb$, $qb$Requesting an independent technical audit to determine whether the error originated from system tools or human input.$qb$, $qb$Focusing communications on the strategic long-term benefits of the new process update to maintain team morale.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The matter could later be reviewed by audit or regulators, and commercial pressure favors the less transparent option. What is the ethically sound action when discovering an entry error that artificially inflates team performance figures?$qb$, $qb$Rectifying the figures to reflect accurate data, documenting verified sources, and informing your lead of the correction.$qb$, $qb$Flagging the data variance to your manager and awaiting explicit instructions before making modifications.$qb$, $qb$Adjusting figures partially to match target goals while noting data collection adjustments in the appendix.$qb$, $qb$Leaving the reported figures unchanged for the current cycle while correcting collection scripts for future reports.$qb$, $qb$Submitting the report as compiled, assuming management evaluates annual rather than monthly productivity trends.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Recognition is uneven across functions, and the way success is credited may affect willingness to collaborate next time. How can a coordinator best foster workplace gratitude and resilience after an intense team project crunch?$qb$, $qb$Recognizing specific peer contributions publicly, facilitating a brief appreciation session, and ensuring rest recovery.$qb$, $qb$Sending a general thank-you email to the department highlighting upcoming quarterly performance goals.$qb$, $qb$Providing a catered team lunch while encouraging members to maintain high productivity for upcoming tasks.$qb$, $qb$Requesting that department heads acknowledge the team's hard work during the upcoming company newsletter.$qb$, $qb$Resuming standard daily routines immediately to help the team return to normal working hours without delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-042$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The disagreement may indicate that the governing standard itself is unclear, outdated, or interpreted differently across teams. What reaction demonstrates high feedback receptivity when a peer suggests communication format improvements?$qb$, $qb$Incorporating feedback gratefully, adopting structured formatting changes, and following up on progress.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledging peer comments politely while explaining why detailed background context was initially included.$qb$, $qb$Shortening future communications significantly by removing background details to keep messages brief.$qb$, $qb$Asking managers during routine check-ins whether they also find your communication updates overly detailed.$qb$, $qb$Accepting peer comments quietly while maintaining current writing habits for external communications.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-043$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Senior leaders must choose between options with different financial, customer, and risk consequences under uncertainty. How can an analyst effectively present complex operational data to non-technical business stakeholders?$qb$, $qb$Framing raw data metrics around a compelling problem-solution narrative that highlights strategic business impacts.$qb$, $qb$Displaying comprehensive financial data tables on presentation slides to ensure complete accuracy.$qb$, $qb$Focusing slides strictly on high-level general summaries, providing spreadsheets as optional add-ons.$qb$, $qb$Delivering a sequential numerical walkthrough, pausing after each data table to ask for questions.$qb$, $qb$Asking a technical colleague to present data tables while you handle introductory and closing remarks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R3-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Teams in several time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially alter scope. What is essential when handing off project updates to global collaborators working in different time zones?$qb$, $qb$Articulating clear context, tagging task owners, providing verified resource links, and flagging potential blockers.$qb$, $qb$Sending a brief summary message stating project progress and asking peers to check shared folders.$qb$, $qb$Scheduling late-night live sync calls to walk global peers through project updates verbally.$qb$, $qb$Uploading updated project files to shared folders, assuming peers will review revision histories independently.$qb$, $qb$Sending voice recordings detailing updates and asking peers to send messages if confusion occurs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The same two people usually get the chance to present team work. The choice affects delivery today, future succession depth, and confidence in how opportunities are allocated. What is the fairest improvement?$qb$, $qb$Rotate presentation opportunities with preparation support$qb$, $qb$Invite volunteers each time and encourage new people$qb$, $qb$Ask the usual presenters to choose their replacements$qb$, $qb$Give the next presentation to the quietest person$qb$, $qb$Keep the current approach because it already works$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You discover that your mistake caused a small delay for another team. Senior leaders know you supported the original course, so your response will establish a visible norm for accountability. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the mistake, explain the fix, and prevent a repeat$qb$, $qb$Tell the affected team and correct the mistake quickly$qb$, $qb$Fix it quietly before anyone asks about the delay$qb$, $qb$Explain the circumstances that contributed to the mistake$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether the delay causes a real problem$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A report contains a figure that makes results look better, but you know the figure is wrong. The issue has governance, commercial, and reputational implications and may later be independently scrutinized. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the figure and record the accurate source$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager about the error before submitting$qb$, $qb$Add a note saying the figure may need checking$qb$, $qb$Leave it for this report and correct it next time$qb$, $qb$Submit it because the difference is small$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate stayed late to help you finish an urgent task. The way credit is handled will influence whether functions are willing to collaborate on the next difficult initiative. What is the most constructive response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for the help and its impact$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately before the next workday$qb$, $qb$Send a general thank-you message to the whole team$qb$, $qb$Assume they know their help was appreciated$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because helping teammates is expected$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-042$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A peer says your updates are too detailed. The disagreement may reveal that the governing standard itself is ambiguous, outdated, or inconsistently interpreted. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Ask which parts are hard to use, then adjust the next update$qb$, $qb$Thank them and try a shorter structure next time$qb$, $qb$Explain why every detail is necessary$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether they agree with the peer$qb$, $qb$Keep the same format unless more people complain$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-043$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You have one minute to explain why a result matters. Senior leaders must choose among options with different financial, customer, risk, and timing consequences. What is the best structure?$qb$, $qb$State the key result, explain its meaning, and give the main takeaway$qb$, $qb$State the result and one supporting fact$qb$, $qb$Read the figures in the order they appear$qb$, $qb$Explain all the background before giving the result$qb$, $qb$Show the full data table and let the listener decide$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R4-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You hand over work to a colleague who will read your message several hours later. Teams across time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially change scope. What should the message include?$qb$, $qb$Context, current status, required action, owner, and deadline$qb$, $qb$Current status, file link, and the next action$qb$, $qb$A short note asking them to call if anything is unclear$qb$, $qb$Only the file link because the document has the details$qb$, $qb$A voice message without a written summary$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team meeting is dominated by two confident speakers. The choice affects immediate delivery, succession depth, and trust in how high-value opportunities are allocated. What can you do to improve participation?$qb$, $qb$Invite views from people who have not yet spoken$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to give one short view before closing the topic$qb$, $qb$Let the discussion continue because anyone can speak$qb$, $qb$Ask the quietest person directly for an answer without warning$qb$, $qb$End the discussion once the confident speakers agree$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate finds an error in a file you prepared. Senior leadership knows you sponsored the original direction, so your response will visibly define the accountability standard. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, correct it, and check whether anything else was affected$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the error and fix the file quickly$qb$, $qb$Explain why the error happened before making the correction$qb$, $qb$Ask them to correct it because they found it$qb$, $qb$Say the error is minor and does not need attention$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker asks you to share a customer file they are not authorized to access. The matter has governance, commercial, and reputational implications and may later face independent scrutiny. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline and direct them to the approved access process$qb$, $qb$Check why they need it before deciding what to do$qb$, $qb$Share only the parts you think are harmless$qb$, $qb$Send it if they promise not to forward it$qb$, $qb$Share it because they work in the same company$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A difficult week ends successfully because several teammates helped one another. How credit is handled will influence future willingness to collaborate across functions. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize specific contributions and also note what the team learned$qb$, $qb$Thank the team and mention the successful result$qb$, $qb$Arrange a small celebration without discussing the work$qb$, $qb$Move straight to the next target while motivation is high$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the mistakes so the team improves$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R5-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You ask a colleague in another time zone to complete a task before your next workday. Teams across time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially change scope. What is the best message?$qb$, $qb$Give the context, exact action, deadline, and link to what they need$qb$, $qb$State the action and deadline with the relevant file attached$qb$, $qb$Send a short request and offer to explain later$qb$, $qb$Send several messages as you remember more details$qb$, $qb$Ask them to call you if they are unsure$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague with a hearing difficulty asks for written actions after meetings. The choice affects immediate performance, succession depth, and confidence in how development opportunities are allocated. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Provide clear written actions as a normal part of the meeting follow-up$qb$, $qb$Send written actions to them after each meeting$qb$, $qb$Ask them to remind you whenever they need notes$qb$, $qb$Suggest that a teammate share personal notes with them$qb$, $qb$Say meetings are mainly verbal and cannot suit everyone$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate suggests a better solution after you have already presented your idea. Senior leaders know you backed the original direction, so your response will visibly model accountability. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate their solution fairly and support it if it is better$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the advantages before deciding$qb$, $qb$Defend your idea first because it was already presented$qb$, $qb$Combine both ideas even if one is clearly weaker$qb$, $qb$Reject the new idea to avoid changing direction$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to approve a check you did not actually perform. The matter has governance, commercial, and reputational implications and may later face independent review. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not approve it until the required check has been completed$qb$, $qb$Explain that you can approve it after checking the work$qb$, $qb$Approve it if a trusted colleague says the work is correct$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the deadline is close$qb$, $qb$Approve it now and check later$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A coworker quietly fixes a data issue before it reaches the client. The way recognition is handled will influence future willingness to collaborate across organizational boundaries. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically and share the useful prevention lesson with the team$qb$, $qb$Thank them and ask how they spotted the issue$qb$, $qb$Say 'good catch' and continue$qb$, $qb$Focus on who created the data issue$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because fixing issues is part of the job$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R6-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague to review a file while you are offline. Teams across time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially change scope. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$Please review sections 2 and 3 by 2 PM, add comments in the file, and message me if a blocker prevents completion$qb$, $qb$Please review sections 2 and 3 by 2 PM and add comments$qb$, $qb$Please review the attached file today$qb$, $qb$Please take a look when you can$qb$, $qb$Can we discuss the file later?$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague cannot attend a regular meeting because of an approved work arrangement. The choice affects current delivery, succession depth, and trust in how high-value opportunities are allocated. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Make key information and decisions available in a way they can access$qb$, $qb$Send them the meeting notes and invite questions$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate to brief them informally$qb$, $qb$Expect them to find out what they missed$qb$, $qb$Remove them from work discussed in that meeting$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You strongly supported a plan, but new information shows another approach is better. Senior leadership knows you backed the original direction, so changing course will visibly set the accountability standard. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the new evidence and support the better approach$qb$, $qb$Review the new information and explain why you are changing your view$qb$, $qb$Continue defending your plan until a manager decides$qb$, $qb$Suggest combining both approaches to avoid admitting a change$qb$, $qb$Ignore the new information$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You notice a small expense has been entered twice in a claim. The matter has governance, commercial, and reputational implications and may later face independent scrutiny. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the duplicate before the claim is submitted$qb$, $qb$Tell the person preparing the claim and ask them to correct it$qb$, $qb$Submit it and correct it if finance notices$qb$, $qb$Leave it because the amount is small$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the duplicate was accidental$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate gives you useful feedback that improves your final work. How recognition is handled will influence whether functions collaborate openly on the next difficult initiative. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for the specific feedback and tell them how it improved the result$qb$, $qb$Thank them and show them the improved version$qb$, $qb$Say thanks and submit the work$qb$, $qb$Avoid mentioning the feedback because the final work is yours$qb$, $qb$Take full credit because you made the change$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R7-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A colleague will pick up your work tomorrow morning. Teams across time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially change scope. Which handover is most useful?$qb$, $qb$Current status, completed work, open issue, next action, owner, and deadline$qb$, $qb$Current status, open issue, and next action$qb$, $qb$A link to the project folder with a short note$qb$, $qb$A message saying 'please continue from here'$qb$, $qb$No message because the files are shared$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team social activity always happens at a time one colleague cannot attend because of a regular commitment. The choice affects immediate delivery, succession depth, and trust in how high-value development opportunities are allocated. What is the best approach?$qb$, $qb$Discuss alternative times or formats so participation is reasonably accessible$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague which other times could work$qb$, $qb$Keep the usual time but send them photos afterward$qb$, $qb$Tell them attendance is optional$qb$, $qb$Exclude them from future planning because they cannot attend$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out that your proposed solution has a serious gap. Senior leadership knows you backed the original direction, so changing course will visibly define the accountability standard. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, examine the gap, and change the solution if the concern is valid$qb$, $qb$Ask them to explain the gap and possible alternatives$qb$, $qb$Defend your solution before considering the concern$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide whose view is correct$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the concern because you prepared the solution$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A friend at work asks you to mark them present at a meeting they did not attend. The matter has governance, commercial, and reputational implications and may later face independent scrutiny. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Refuse and keep the attendance record accurate$qb$, $qb$Explain that you cannot change the attendance record$qb$, $qb$Mark them present if they had a good reason for missing it$qb$, $qb$Leave the attendance field blank$qb$, $qb$Mark them present because it has no business impact$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate shares a template that makes your work much easier. How recognition is handled will influence whether functions collaborate openly on the next difficult initiative. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically and share how the template helped your work$qb$, $qb$Thank them and ask whether the template can be shared with others$qb$, $qb$Use the template and say thanks later$qb$, $qb$Share the template without mentioning who created it$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because templates are meant to be reused$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R8-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You finish your work late at night and a teammate will continue in the morning. Teams across time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially change scope. What is the best handover?$qb$, $qb$Leave a concise update with status, open issue, next action, and links to the current files$qb$, $qb$State what is complete and what they should do next$qb$, $qb$Send the file links without an explanation$qb$, $qb$Send several messages describing everything you did$qb$, $qb$Wait until morning to explain it live$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team member needs captions to follow an online training session. The choice affects current delivery, succession depth, and confidence in access to future opportunities. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Use an accessible session or enable accurate captions so they can participate fully$qb$, $qb$Enable available captions and check whether they work for the person$qb$, $qb$Send the recording afterward$qb$, $qb$Ask a teammate to summarize the training for them$qb$, $qb$Tell them to attend and read the slides$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You discover that a teammate's suggestion would fix a problem better than the solution you proposed. Senior leaders know you backed the original direction, so changing course will visibly model accountability. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Support the better solution and openly credit the teammate's contribution$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate you agree and use their suggestion$qb$, $qb$Combine both solutions so your idea remains included$qb$, $qb$Ask a manager which solution should be used$qb$, $qb$Keep your own solution because you proposed it first$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$You are asked to copy a signature from an old document onto a new form to save time. The matter has governance, commercial, and reputational implications and may later face independent scrutiny. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Refuse and obtain the required approval or signature through the proper process$qb$, $qb$Explain that the new form needs proper authorization$qb$, $qb$Copy it only if the same person would approve the form$qb$, $qb$Ask a coworker whether this is normally done$qb$, $qb$Copy it because the content is routine$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices a risk early and prevents rework for the whole group. The way success is credited will influence future cross-functional cooperation. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the specific action and explain the rework it prevented$qb$, $qb$Thank them and tell the team what was avoided$qb$, $qb$Say 'well done' in the team chat$qb$, $qb$Move on because the problem never occurred$qb$, $qb$Focus on why the risk existed$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R9-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A teammate in another time zone needs to make a decision before you return online. Teams across time zones must continue while you are unavailable, including a decision that could materially change scope. Which message is best?$qb$, $qb$State the decision needed, options, key facts, deadline, and what they may decide without you$qb$, $qb$State the decision, options, and deadline clearly$qb$, $qb$Send the background file and ask them to decide$qb$, $qb$Write 'please decide urgently'$qb$, $qb$Wait until you are both online$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Promotion data shows women progress at similar rates overall, but one critical feeder role has almost no representation from two underrepresented groups. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Examine the feeder pipeline, selection criteria and access to qualifying assignments, then address barriers supported by evidence.$qb$, $qb$Set a representation target for the feeder role and monitor it quarterly.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to nominate more diverse candidates for the next promotion cycle.$qb$, $qb$Treat the overall promotion rate as sufficient evidence that the process is fair.$qb$, $qb$Lower the role requirements so more people from underrepresented groups qualify.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A project you championed is succeeding commercially, but a design shortcut you approved is now causing avoidable rework for another function. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge your role in the shortcut, quantify the downstream cost, and lead a correction that balances current value with future impact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the affected function to document the rework so the project team can respond.$qb$, $qb$Defend the shortcut because the commercial outcome validates the decision overall.$qb$, $qb$Let the project manager decide whether the issue is material enough to fix.$qb$, $qb$Avoid reopening the decision because doing so may undermine confidence in the project.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A senior customer offers confidential competitor pricing if you agree to keep the source off the record. The information would materially strengthen your bid. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not use improperly obtained information; document the approach and follow the appropriate legal or compliance route.$qb$, $qb$Decline the pricing details but continue the commercial discussion with the customer.$qb$, $qb$Ask whether the information is already public before deciding whether to use it.$qb$, $qb$Use the information only to check whether your current price is broadly competitive.$qb$, $qb$Accept the information because the customer, not your company, is responsible for sharing it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team worked through a difficult launch and delivered on time, but one subgroup carried a disproportionate burden. Which response is most constructive after go-live?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the contribution specifically, correct the workload imbalance, and capture what should change before the next launch.$qb$, $qb$Thank the entire team publicly and give the subgroup additional time off.$qb$, $qb$Reward the subgroup financially if budget allows and then return to normal operations.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing the imbalance because highlighting it may create resentment.$qb$, $qb$Celebrate the success only; post-launch strain is part of high-performance work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-10TO17-R10-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A 24-hour operations team hands work across three time zones. A policy exception can be approved locally up to a threshold; above it, a director must decide. What should the handoff include?$qb$, $qb$Current state, evidence, threshold position, decision needed, authority available to the next team, and the exact trigger for escalation.$qb$, $qb$A concise status note, the relevant files, and the name of the director on call.$qb$, $qb$The full activity log so the next team can reconstruct the case independently.$qb$, $qb$A message saying the issue is urgent and may need director approval.$qb$, $qb$Only the latest status because the next team can ask questions if needed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Promotion rates for one demographic group are materially lower despite comparable performance and tenure, with no obvious single-manager pattern. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Examine decision stages, criteria, opportunity access and calibration for systemic barriers, then correct mechanisms and monitor outcomes.$qb$, $qb$Remind managers that promotion decisions must be unbiased.$qb$, $qb$Set a quota immediately to equalize percentages.$qb$, $qb$Review only managers with the lowest rates for that group.$qb$, $qb$Assume the difference reflects preferences unless employees complain.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An acquisition you championed is missing its synergy case although integration leaders followed the approved plan. What response sets the strongest standard?$qb$, $qb$Own the thesis and assumptions, run a fact-based reset, change choices where needed, and make recovery accountability explicit.$qb$, $qb$Ask integration leaders why execution missed the synergies.$qb$, $qb$Defend the acquisition logic while replacing the integration plan.$qb$, $qb$Wait another year before judging the thesis.$qb$, $qb$Distance yourself because the board approved the transaction.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A public official hints that a donation to a legitimate foundation would accelerate a permit critical to expansion. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not link any donation to the permit; document the interaction and route both matters through independent compliance processes.$qb$, $qb$Decline now and reconsider the donation after the permit is issued.$qb$, $qb$Make a small donation within normal giving limits.$qb$, $qb$Ask a local adviser whether such donations are customary.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the recipient is a legitimate charity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A division delivered exceptional results through an exhausting turnaround, but employees say the pace was unsustainable. What is the most constructive response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the achievement while acknowledging the cost and changing conditions that made chronic overextension necessary.$qb$, $qb$Celebrate the results first and discuss workload separately later.$qb$, $qb$Give the team additional leave without revisiting the operating model.$qb$, $qb$Recognize only the highest performers who carried the largest load.$qb$, $qb$Avoid celebrating because praise could endorse unhealthy practices.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R01-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global product decision must be made overnight across three time zones. The final approver will be offline when the technical team finishes analysis. What handoff is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Record the decision required, current evidence, unresolved risks, thresholds, recommendation, authority available and exact conditions for escalation.$qb$, $qb$Send the latest analysis and ask the approver to call if questions arise.$qb$, $qb$Leave a detailed chronological log of all discussions.$qb$, $qb$Send a short message saying the decision is urgent.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the approver is online so accountability is unambiguous.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An executive talent program has balanced applications but sharply unequal final selection outcomes. What should leadership examine first?$qb$, $qb$Examine final-stage evidence, criteria, calibration and sponsorship effects for barriers that are not visible earlier in the process.$qb$, $qb$Take a practical partial step and escalate the remaining uncertainty to a higher authority.$qb$, $qb$Apply the existing policy more carefully, even though it does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects the most visible short-term target.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the conflicting evidence and continue with the established position.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major initiative you sponsored fails after a risk you consciously accepted materializes; the delivery team followed the approved plan. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Own the risk decision, explain the reasoning without defensiveness, lead recovery, and update the decision framework from what occurred.$qb$, $qb$Act on the strongest available signal while keeping a later review point.$qb$, $qb$Seek broader consensus before making a limited decision.$qb$, $qb$Use a familiar precedent even though important conditions differ.$qb$, $qb$Choose the easiest path to implement regardless of longer-term consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A politically connected intermediary offers to 'solve' a licensing delay for a success fee but will not explain how. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline the opaque arrangement, document it, conduct appropriate due diligence, and use transparent lawful channels.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team delivered a critical recovery through repeated weekend work, and leaders want that effort to become the new benchmark. What is the best response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the exceptional contribution while treating it as exceptional, then remove conditions that made repeated sacrifice necessary.$qb$, $qb$Introduce a sensible safeguard but leave the underlying incentives or decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Run a small corrective action focused mainly on the most visible symptom.$qb$, $qb$Ask individual managers to compensate for the problem through extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Treat the issue as unavoidable and make no material change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R02-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global operations team hands critical work across time zones, but decisions are repeatedly reopened because context is lost. What should leadership establish?$qb$, $qb$Use a decision record capturing current state, rationale, evidence, owner, open assumptions and conditions for reopening, integrated into handoffs.$qb$, $qb$Resolve the immediate pressure and document the remaining risk for later review.$qb$, $qb$Use a narrower solution that improves one dimension but leaves key dependencies unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Transfer the decision to another function without clarifying enterprise ownership.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current plan even when evidence shows its assumptions are weakening.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$High-potential nominations disproportionately come from headquarters because senior sponsors know those employees better. What is the strongest redesign?$qb$, $qb$Broaden evidence and sponsorship routes, calibrate against common criteria, and monitor whether location still affects opportunity independent of merit.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your division exceeds target partly because another division absorbed an unplanned cost created by your decision. The board congratulates you. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Make the transferred cost visible, explain enterprise economics, and correct the performance interpretation even though it reduces your apparent result.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An acquisition target asks you to omit a known compliance investigation from an early board briefing because nothing is proven. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ensure the investigation and uncertainty are disclosed through proper diligence and governance so decision-makers are not misled.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue, but leave the wider assumptions and ownership largely unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current approach and ask affected teams to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until the problem becomes unambiguous or external pressure forces action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cost program succeeded because several teams helped outside their remit, but only the program office is being recognized. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize specific cross-boundary contributions and reinforce the conditions that enabled cooperation, not just visible ownership.$qb$, $qb$Address the most urgent part of the issue and schedule the broader questions for a later review.$qb$, $qb$Use an existing policy or precedent even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the decision upward without first clarifying the trade-offs.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that protects current targets regardless of wider consequences.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R03-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An operations redesign improves average throughput but creates severe delays for a small class of high-value cases. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment the flow by value and variability, identify why the redesign harms those cases, and adjust routing or capacity without discarding gains for the majority.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core design and incentives intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask the main stakeholders for more input but do not establish how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term decision primarily to restore speed.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the status quo because changing it could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A leadership slate contains diverse candidates, but informal 'readiness' discussions consistently favor people resembling previous incumbents. What should be done?$qb$, $qb$Replace vague readiness judgments with explicit evidence and calibrated criteria, challenge pattern-matching assumptions, and review outcomes for unexplained disparities.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A transformation you lead succeeds, but an unpopular decision made by a departing executive was crucial to the result. How should you handle recognition?$qb$, $qb$Make their material contribution visible alongside others' work, regardless of current popularity, so credit reflects causal contribution rather than organizational status.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A customer asks your company to certify that a control was operating throughout a period when evidence exists for only part of it. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$State only what the evidence supports, disclose the limitation, and use the proper remediation or assurance process rather than extending the claim.$qb$, $qb$Improve the existing approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Ask stakeholders for additional views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term compromise that reduces visible tension without addressing the underlying mechanism.$qb$, $qb$Continue the status quo because changing it could introduce new uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$After a difficult integration, employees credit one highly visible leader while less-visible teams carried much of the operational burden. What is the strongest recognition approach?$qb$, $qb$Recognize contributions in proportion to their actual impact, including less-visible work, and connect appreciation to the collaborative behaviors the organization wants repeated.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate concern with a sensible local action, then revisit the wider implications at the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more input and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central ownership or trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current design and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Avoid changing course until external evidence makes the problem indisputable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R04-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An efficiency redesign improves average cycle time but makes rare critical cases much slower. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment demand by consequence and variability, identify why critical cases degrade, and redesign routing or capacity while preserving gains for routine work.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing policy or precedent and add a modest safeguard for this case.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior decision without first clarifying the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest option to restore momentum, even if wider consequences remain unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position and leave secondary impacts to other owners.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Employees with caregiving responsibilities are underrepresented in a prestigious assignment because it assumes extensive travel, although much work can be done remotely. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Reassess which travel is genuinely essential, redesign delivery where feasible, and keep selection based on capability rather than an avoidable access barrier.$qb$, $qb$At first, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$In this case, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A board paper you approved contains a material analytical error discovered hours before the meeting. Correcting it may delay an important decision. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the error before the decision, explain its impact and the assurance failure, and distinguish whether the recommendation still holds under accurate analysis.$qb$, $qb$In this case, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$At first, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A sales executive proposes recording revenue before contractual conditions are met to avoid missing guidance, arguing it will reverse next quarter anyway. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reject premature recognition, ensure reporting follows applicable rules and evidence, and escalate through proper financial governance if pressure continues.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, improve the current approach incrementally while keeping its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, seek more stakeholder views but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$At first, make a unilateral short-term decision that reduces visible tension but leaves the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$In this case, avoid intervention because changing the current model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A difficult turnaround succeeded partly because employees challenged several of your initial assumptions. What is the most constructive recognition?$qb$, $qb$Credit the people and behaviors that improved the outcome, including respectful challenge, and make clear that changing leadership's mind on evidence is valued.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, take a useful immediate step on the main issue and schedule the broader structural question for a later governance review.$qb$, $qb$At first, gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$In this case, preserve the current framework and ask the affected leaders to absorb or manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, maintain the existing course until the risk becomes certain enough to force a decision.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R05-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process redesign reduces cost per transaction but sharply increases abandonment among complex cases. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment cases by complexity and value, identify where the new design creates friction, and create differentiated routing while preserving efficient handling for routine demand.$qb$, $qb$In this case, apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though the precedent does not fully address the situation.$qb$, $qb$As an interim response, escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$For the immediate decision, choose the quickest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$At first, protect the current target or position even if the cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A global mobility program is open to everyone, but assignments are usually filled through leaders' networks before vacancies are widely known. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Create transparent access and selection routes, require evidence against common criteria, and monitor whether informal sponsorship still determines opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral decision that reduces visible tension while leaving the underlying mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A transformation misses its target because assumptions you approved were too optimistic, although execution was strong. What should you tell the board?$qb$, $qb$Own the assumption error, distinguish it from execution, explain revised evidence and recovery choices, and strengthen future assumption challenge.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A potential partner offers valuable data that appears to have been collected without valid customer consent. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not use it until provenance and lawful use are established; investigate through proper privacy and legal channels and redesign the opportunity if needed.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and schedule the broader structural question for the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment, but leave the central trade-off or ownership unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the resulting consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current course until the risk becomes certain enough to force action.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A turnaround is complete, and one visible team receives most praise while several enabling teams carried substantial hidden work. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Recognize specific visible and enabling contributions, explain how they combined to create the result, and avoid equating visibility with value.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it does not fully fit the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before working through the competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise to restore momentum without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R06-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An operations redesign improves average throughput but creates severe delays for rare high-value cases. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment the flow by value and variability, identify why those cases degrade, and adjust routing or capacity while preserving gains for routine work.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer the less visible consequences until results can be measured.$qb$, $qb$Use a partial pilot without specifying the evidence or threshold that would determine the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operational team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects this year's reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A development program requires two years at headquarters, excluding many strong employees who cannot relocate, although most work can be done elsewhere. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which experiences genuinely require physical presence, redesign access around required outcomes, and retain relocation only where it adds material developmental value.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its main incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek more stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A division you lead benefited from a favorable accounting assumption that you questioned privately but did not challenge before results were published. What should you do now?$qb$, $qb$Raise the issue promptly, correct any misleading interpretation, own your failure to challenge it earlier, and strengthen the review process for future judgments.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot but leave the success threshold and next decision undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A prospective government customer suggests that hiring a relative of a decision-maker would 'help everyone feel comfortable' with your bid. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline any employment linkage to the procurement decision, document the suggestion, and route the matter through appropriate compliance and bidding channels.$qb$, $qb$Take a useful immediate step on the visible issue, but defer the wider structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A cross-functional team prevented a major loss by quietly sharing resources despite conflicting formal targets. What should leadership recognize?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the specific collaborative actions and impact, then correct the targets that made helpful behavior depend on informal workarounds.$qb$, $qb$Use the closest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a senior ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if risk or cost is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R07-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$An operations network meets average service targets, but one region experiences chronic volatility because demand is highly seasonal. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment the operating model around demand variability, redesign capacity and routing for the seasonal pattern, and avoid using network averages to mask persistent local failure.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot but leave the success threshold and next decision undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A prestigious assignment requires frequent late-evening calls across time zones although most could be scheduled differently. Participation from some employee groups is low. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Redesign the operating cadence around genuinely necessary overlap, preserve role standards, and remove avoidable scheduling barriers before judging candidate availability.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategy you personally advocated is no longer supported by new evidence, but reversing it will be publicly embarrassing. What is the strongest response?$qb$, $qb$Change course transparently, explain what evidence changed the judgment, own the earlier decision, and show that updating on evidence is expected leadership behavior.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A distributor suggests routing payments through an unrelated consulting company to speed customs clearance. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline the opaque payment route, document the request, investigate through appropriate compliance channels, and use legitimate customs processes.$qb$, $qb$Take a sensible immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next governance review.$qb$, $qb$Gather more information and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A difficult year ends with acceptable results because employees repeatedly helped teams outside their formal goals. How should leadership respond?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the specific cross-team contribution and then adjust structures or measures so future cooperation does not depend on employees acting against formal incentives.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, although it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R08-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A process redesign reduces average inventory but creates frequent stockouts for products with volatile demand. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment inventory policy by demand variability and consequence, redesign buffers and replenishment for volatile items, and preserve efficiency where stable demand supports it.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A high-profile assignment is nominally open to all, but selection requires sponsorship from one of six executives who know only a small portion of the workforce. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Create additional evidence-based access routes, calibrate selection independently of sponsor familiarity, and monitor whether sponsorship remains a gate unrelated to capability.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A business result you reported as a success is later shown to have benefited from a one-off factor your team overlooked. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the interpretation, distinguish recurring performance from the one-off effect, own the analytical oversight, and update how future results are validated.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A supplier offers a substantial personal hospitality package while you are deciding whether to renew its contract. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline or handle it strictly under applicable policy, disclose the conflict as required, and keep the commercial decision demonstrably independent of personal benefit.$qb$, $qb$Take a reasonable immediate action on the visible issue and defer the broader structural question to the next formal review.$qb$, $qb$Gather additional evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage its consequences.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until the risk becomes unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A team delivered a difficult program by lending its best people to another function, causing its own local metrics to suffer. How should leadership respond?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the enterprise contribution and adjust performance interpretation so people are not penalized for value-creating cooperation that leadership needed.$qb$, $qb$Apply the nearest existing precedent with an added safeguard, even though it only partly fits the situation.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining the competing assumptions.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quickest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target even if costs or risks are transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R09-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A distribution network reduces average transport cost but creates fragile dependence on one hub. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate efficiency alongside concentration and recovery exposure, then redesign routing, buffers or contingency capacity according to the value of resilience.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A high-potential program requires international travel at short notice, although many assignments could be planned earlier. Some strong employees therefore cannot participate. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Distinguish genuinely unpredictable travel from poor planning, redesign scheduling where feasible, and keep access based on capability rather than avoidable availability barriers.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current approach incrementally while leaving its core incentives and decision structure intact.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder views but do not define how conflicting evidence will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a short-term unilateral choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing the model could create more uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
