  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-17TO25-R10-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A warehouse network cuts inventory overall but experiences repeated shortages of low-volume critical parts. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Segment inventory by variability and consequence, set differentiated buffers and replenishment rules, and preserve leaner policies where the risk profile supports them.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate concern and defer less visible consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a limited pilot without defining the evidence threshold for the next decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask the operating team to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R01-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your succession process produces diverse shortlists but final appointments repeatedly favor candidates with careers similar to current executives. What is the strongest intervention?$qb$, $qb$Examine how final-stage evidence, sponsorship and definitions of readiness reproduce the incumbent profile, recalibrate criteria, and hold decision-makers accountable for unexplained patterns.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
