  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A succession committee repeatedly discounts leaders from smaller countries because they have not managed enough revenue, despite exceptional complexity and influence. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess scope through complexity, strategic impact, stakeholder range and decision consequence rather than revenue alone, and recalibrate readiness evidence accordingly.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic bet you championed produces strong revenue but creates a dependency that now threatens the company's bargaining power. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the unintended strategic cost, reassess the thesis beyond revenue, and invest or negotiate to reduce dependency even if doing so lowers near-term returns.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A powerful intermediary offers access to senior officials but refuses to disclose its owners or how fees are distributed. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not engage until ownership, role, payments and conflicts are transparently established through appropriate diligence, regardless of the commercial opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A company celebrates record performance, but much of it came from employees absorbing repeated disruption and role uncertainty. What is the strongest leadership response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the contribution and cost honestly, reward where appropriate, and address the organizational conditions that made exceptional personal absorption necessary.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R06-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your executive team works asynchronously across continents, but decisions sometimes depend on unstated local context. What should you establish?$qb$, $qb$Require decision proposals to surface relevant context, assumptions and constraints, invite region-specific challenge, and move materially context-dependent issues to richer discussion before closure.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R07-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A succession committee discounts leaders from turnaround roles because their businesses have not shown sustained growth, despite exceptional recovery performance. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Assess the capabilities and complexity demonstrated in context, distinguish inherited conditions from leadership contribution, and calibrate readiness against future-role demands rather than headline growth.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R07-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic initiative you defended for years is now surviving mainly because teams fear telling you it should stop. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Create an independent review, explicitly remove any expectation to defend the initiative, examine disconfirming evidence and stop or redirect it if the original thesis no longer holds.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R07-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A politically exposed customer asks for unusually favorable payment terms and hints that regulatory relationships could improve in return. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep commercial terms independent of regulatory influence, document the implication, apply appropriate diligence and approval, and reject any quid pro quo.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R07-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major strategic success is being attributed to a few senior leaders although thousands of employees absorbed operational disruption to enable it. What is the strongest recognition?$qb$, $qb$Recognize leadership and broad operational contribution with specificity, acknowledge the burden carried, and ensure the story of success reflects collective institutional effort.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R07-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your global executive team uses asynchronous approvals, but people sometimes approve based only on the latest comment rather than the full decision context. What should you establish?$qb$, $qb$Require concise decision context, alternatives, material changes and final rationale in one authoritative record, with renewed review when the proposal changes materially.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A succession process favors leaders who inherited large businesses over those who built smaller businesses from difficult starting points. What is strongest?$qb$, $qb$Evaluate leadership contribution, complexity, trajectory and transferable capability rather than inherited scale alone, and calibrate readiness against future-role demands.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major strategy you sponsored is performing poorly, but changing it now would make several years of prior investment appear wasted. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Ignore sunk-cost optics, reassess future value from current evidence, own the earlier choices, and stop or redirect resources if the remaining thesis no longer justifies them.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A potential customer offers access to a large contract if your company employs a consultant closely related to a public official. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Treat the arrangement as a potential conflict, decline any improper linkage, conduct appropriate diligence and keep procurement and employment decisions independently governed.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A successful restructuring is being celebrated while many employees who enabled it are leaving because their roles disappeared. What is the strongest leadership response?$qb$, $qb$Recognize their contribution and transition honestly, provide appropriate support, and avoid a success narrative that erases the people who bore much of the change's cost.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R08-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your executive team uses asynchronous decision documents, but minority views disappear once the final version is edited. What should you establish?$qb$, $qb$Preserve material dissent and the evidence behind it in the decision record, along with final rationale and review triggers, so closure does not erase useful uncertainty.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A succession process values experience running large established businesses, but future strategy depends heavily on building new markets. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Rebalance readiness evidence toward ambiguity, creation, ecosystem building and scaling while retaining necessary stewardship capabilities, rather than reproducing the profile of past roles.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic investment you sponsored is still profitable but evidence shows a better use of the capital now exists. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reallocate based on current opportunity cost and strategy, explain why profitability alone does not justify continued capital, and avoid protecting the investment because of personal sponsorship.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-009$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A government-linked customer asks you to route a legitimate consulting payment through a third party for 'administrative convenience.' What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Require transparent contracting, beneficial-ownership and payment purpose verification, and decline any route that cannot withstand normal compliance scrutiny.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A company achieves a historic milestone, but several teams that laid the foundation years earlier were reorganized away. What is the strongest recognition?$qb$, $qb$Credit the institutional chain of contribution, including predecessor teams where appropriate, so recognition reflects how value was actually built rather than only who is present today.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R09-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$Your global team makes asynchronous decisions well, but local regulatory nuances sometimes appear only after approval. What should you establish?$qb$, $qb$Require explicit local-context checks and named regional input for affected decisions, with reopening rules when material jurisdictional facts emerge.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-008$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A strategic investment you championed remains profitable but is consuming scarce capital needed for a more promising future platform. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Reallocate based on current strategic opportunity cost, explain why positive returns alone do not justify continued capital, and avoid protecting the investment because it is associated with your legacy.$qb$, $qb$Address the largest immediate risk and defer less visible enterprise consequences until the next planning cycle.$qb$, $qb$Run a bounded pilot but leave success criteria and the next decision threshold undefined.$qb$, $qb$Ask relevant operating leaders to absorb the issue within existing authority and resources.$qb$, $qb$Choose the option that best protects current reported performance and reputation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-009$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A politically connected intermediary proposes a legitimate service but insists that part of its fee be paid to an undisclosed offshore entity. What should you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not proceed until beneficial ownership, payment purpose and legitimacy are transparently established through appropriate diligence and compliance review.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-010$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$The company reaches a historic milestone after decades of work across multiple leadership generations. What is the strongest way to recognize it?$qb$, $qb$Credit the institutional chain of contribution, including foundational work no longer visible, connect achievement to shared capabilities and avoid centering recognition only on current leaders.$qb$, $qb$Apply the closest existing precedent with added safeguards, although it does not fully address the strategic context.$qb$, $qb$Escalate for a higher-level ruling before fully examining competing assumptions and consequences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the fastest workable compromise without addressing the underlying driver.$qb$, $qb$Protect the current target or position even if material risk is transferred elsewhere.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-011$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A structural geopolitical shift may force your company to choose between two technology ecosystems that are becoming incompatible. What should leadership do?$qb$, $qb$Map dependencies and option loss, build selective interoperability and regional alternatives, define geopolitical signposts and delay irreversible alignment where strategic flexibility still has value.$qb$, $qb$Improve the current model incrementally while retaining its core incentives, assumptions and decision structure.$qb$, $qb$Seek additional stakeholder input but do not establish how contradictory evidence or interests will be resolved.$qb$, $qb$Make a unilateral short-term choice that reduces visible tension but leaves the systemic mechanism unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Avoid intervention because changing an established model could create additional uncertainty.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-MCQ-25PLUS-R10-045$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$MCQ$qb$, $qb$A major innovation succeeds commercially but reveals governance gaps that would be dangerous at scale. What should the CEO do?$qb$, $qb$Separate product-market success from readiness to scale, address material governance and operating risks proportionately, and expand in stages rather than letting commercial momentum bypass institutional safeguards.$qb$, $qb$Take a credible immediate action on the visible issue and defer broader institutional implications to the next governance cycle.$qb$, $qb$Gather more evidence and make a limited adjustment without resolving the central enterprise trade-off.$qb$, $qb$Preserve the current framework and ask affected leaders to manage the consequences within their areas.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the existing course until external events make change unavoidable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-007$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During team project assignments, you notice that high-visibility client presentations are consistently given to the same two outspoken team members. How do you encourage balanced opportunity?$qb$, $qb$Institute a rotating presentation framework, offer coaching support for newer or quieter team members, and pair junior staff with experienced presenters.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers during the next meeting, giving priority to team members who have not presented recently.$qb$, $qb$Directly assign the next high-visibility presentation to a quieter team member to give them immediate exposure.$qb$, $qb$Discuss the presentation rotation idea with your manager during your next 1-on-1 before making any team changes.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current presentation pairing for critical clients, but allocate internal team meeting presentations to quieter members.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-008$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process update you designed and implemented leads to unexpected delays in daily report generation for another team. How do you handle this mistake?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the issue openly with the affected team, revert or patch the flawed step immediately, and document lessons learned to refine the process.$qb$, $qb$Apologize to the affected team lead and offer to manually process their reports until the system performance stabilizes.$qb$, $qb$Explain to the affected team that initial performance hiccups are standard when deploying new process updates.$qb$, $qb$Ask a technical colleague to look into the system metrics quietly to determine if the delay was caused by your update.$qb$, $qb$Focus on highlighting the long-term strategic benefits of your new process update during the next department sync.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-010$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Your team successfully finishes an exhausting two-week client audit under heavy deadline pressure. Morale is low due to overtime hours worked. How do you help restore team energy?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge individual and collective contributions explicitly in team channels, organize a brief informal thank-you gathering, and ensure compensatory rest time.$qb$, $qb$Send a general email thanking everyone for their hard work and reminding them of upcoming regular project deadlines.$qb$, $qb$Treat the team to a catered lunch while encouraging them to maintain this high productivity level for next month's goals.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to mention the team's successful audit completion during the upcoming department newsletter.$qb$, $qb$Resume standard daily routines immediately so team members can return to regular working hours without dwell time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-042$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A peer gives you constructive feedback stating that your email updates are overly verbose, making key action items hard to identify. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Thank your peer genuinely, review past email examples together, adopt structured bulleted formats going forward, and check back for improvement.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the comment politely, explaining that thorough details are necessary to prevent misunderstandings.$qb$, $qb$Shorten future emails significantly by omitting background context to keep messages as brief as possible.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager during your next check-in if they also find your email updates too long.$qb$, $qb$Accept the feedback quietly, but continue your current writing style when communicating with external clients.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-043$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are presenting complex operational cost data to non-finance stakeholders. How do you structure your deck to ensure engagement and comprehension?$qb$, $qb$Frame raw metrics around a compelling problem-solution arc, highlight strategic business impacts visually, and connect data directly to shared goals.$qb$, $qb$Present complete financial data tables on slides to ensure full transparency and detailed accuracy.$qb$, $qb$Focus presentation slides strictly on high-level summaries, providing detailed spreadsheets as optional annexes.$qb$, $qb$Deliver a standard numerical walk-through, pausing after each table to ask if stakeholders have questions.$qb$, $qb$Ask a finance team colleague to present data tables while you handle introduction and closing slides.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-044$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are handing off a project update to a global colleague in a different time zone. How do you ensure smooth, independent progress while you sleep?$qb$, $qb$Provide a comprehensive context summary, link validated document sources, outline explicit next steps with clear owner tagging, and flag potential blockers.$qb$, $qb$Send a quick summary message stating project progress and ask them to check shared files for details.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a brief late-night sync call to walk them through project updates verbally before their workday starts.$qb$, $qb$Upload updated project files to the shared folder, assuming your colleague will review revision histories.$qb$, $qb$Send a voice note detailing updates and ask them to message you if they encounter any confusion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-053$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A short-term assignment offers strong exposure to leadership. The manager wants to give it to the same experienced employee who handled the last two similar assignments. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm the capability requirements, consider other suitable employees fairly, and provide reasonable support if a less-experienced person can perform the assignment safely.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers and choose the person who appears most confident.$qb$, $qb$Give it to the experienced employee because the assignment is visible and important.$qb$, $qb$Rotate the assignment automatically to someone who has not done it before.$qb$, $qb$Let the manager choose without discussing how repeated selection affects development opportunities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-054$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You recommended a process change that has increased rework. A teammate had raised a concern before launch, but you discounted it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the missed concern, stop or adjust the change based on evidence, credit the teammate's input, and review how future concerns will be tested.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team the change needs adjustment and ask for improvement ideas.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the negative result could not have been known before launch.$qb$, $qb$Quietly reverse the change without revisiting the earlier decision.$qb$, $qb$Keep the change for another month so there is more data before admitting it may be wrong.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-056$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A difficult client delivery succeeds because another department repeatedly helped your team at short notice. The extra effort was not visible to senior leaders. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the specific help directly and in the appropriate leadership forum, explain its impact, and discuss how to make the cooperation easier next time.$qb$, $qb$Send the other department a thank-you email after the project closes.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to mention the other department if the project is praised.$qb$, $qb$Thank only the department head because they allowed the support.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because cross-department support is part of normal work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-088$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague disagrees with feedback you gave and shows evidence that their approach may satisfy the requirement. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the evidence against the requirement, clarify the standard together, and revise your feedback if their reasoning is stronger.$qb$, $qb$Explain your interpretation again and ask them to follow it this time.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide whose interpretation is correct.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw the feedback so the disagreement does not affect the relationship.$qb$, $qb$Keep the feedback unchanged because you were asked to review the work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-089$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You must recommend whether to continue a pilot. It improved turnaround time but increased complaint rates slightly. How do you present the case?$qb$, $qb$Frame the decision, quantify both benefits and drawbacks, explain the trade-off and uncertainty, and recommend the next action with supporting evidence.$qb$, $qb$Show all pilot metrics and let leaders choose.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the time improvement because that was the main pilot objective.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the complaint increase because customer impact is most important.$qb$, $qb$Give your recommendation first and avoid discussing conflicting evidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-090$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone must continue your project while you are offline and may need to make one decision. What do you leave them?$qb$, $qb$A concise handover with current status, source links, next actions, decision boundaries, the condition that requires escalation, and the deadline.$qb$, $qb$A long message describing everything completed during your day.$qb$, $qb$The updated files with a note saying 'please continue.'$qb$, $qb$A request to wait until you are online before making any decision.$qb$, $qb$A voice message without written links or action ownership.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-099$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring client-facing task is always given to employees who speak with a particular accent because leaders believe clients prefer it, although performance data does not support the assumption. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Challenge the unsupported assumption, use job-relevant performance criteria, and give qualified employees fair access to the work.$qb$, $qb$Rotate the task equally among everyone regardless of skill.$qb$, $qb$Keep the current approach because client comfort matters.$qb$, $qb$Ask clients which accents they prefer before assigning work.$qb$, $qb$Give the task only to the most senior employees.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-100$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a mistake in your analysis caused a colleague to spend several hours on unnecessary work. The final deliverable is still on time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague promptly, take responsibility, help correct the wasted work, and adjust your checking method to prevent recurrence.$qb$, $qb$Apologize privately and move on because the final deadline is unaffected.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the source data was confusing.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the project ends to discuss what happened.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the unnecessary work has already been completed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-102$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague shares a simple improvement that saves your team several hours each month. The improvement was outside their formal responsibilities. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the contribution specifically, share the impact with the team and appropriate manager, and make sure the improvement is incorporated sustainably.$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague privately and start using the improvement.$qb$, $qb$Mention the idea in a team meeting without naming the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to document it before offering any recognition.$qb$, $qb$Treat it as normal initiative expected from everyone.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-134$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need to tell a respected peer that their handovers are thorough but consistently arrive too late for your team to use them effectively. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use specific examples, explain the downstream impact, acknowledge the quality of their work, and agree a timing expectation and practical handoff method.$qb$, $qb$Tell them their handovers need to arrive earlier.$qb$, $qb$Copy their manager on the next late handover.$qb$, $qb$Ask your team to adapt its schedule to the peer's timing.$qb$, $qb$Stop giving them feedback because they may take it personally.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-136$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need a colleague in another time zone to make progress on a sensitive task while you sleep. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Leave written context, current status, approved sources, clear next actions, decision limits, escalation conditions, and a deadline so they can act safely without waiting for you.$qb$, $qb$Send them the latest files and ask them to message if they need anything.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a late-night call so you can explain everything live.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to do only routine work until you return.$qb$, $qb$Send several separate chat messages as you remember details.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-143$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks for names for a high-visibility client assignment and suggests choosing only people who have presented to clients before. Several capable colleagues have never had that opportunity. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Suggest selecting against the actual assignment requirements, include capable people without prior exposure, and provide preparation or pairing support where needed.$qb$, $qb$Add one less-experienced colleague to the shortlist alongside the usual experienced presenters.$qb$, $qb$Use experienced presenters for this client and promise newer colleagues an internal presentation opportunity later.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers and choose only from those who put themselves forward.$qb$, $qb$Accept the manager's approach because important client work is not the right place to broaden opportunity.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-144$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A scheduling change you recommended has increased customer waiting time instead of reducing it. The team is reviewing the first month of results. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the result is worse than expected, revisit the assumptions and data with the team, and recommend whether to reverse, adjust, or retest the change.$qb$, $qb$Say the result is disappointing and suggest returning to the previous schedule while you investigate further.$qb$, $qb$Give the change another month before judging because new processes need time to settle.$qb$, $qb$Emphasize that several colleagues did not follow the new schedule consistently, so the idea has not been properly tested.$qb$, $qb$Avoid referring to your original recommendation and let the manager decide the next step.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-146$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague quietly finds and fixes an error in your work before it reaches a customer. Later, the team assumes you solved the problem yourself. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague directly and make their contribution clear when the resolution is discussed with the team.$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately and offer to help them the next time they need support.$qb$, $qb$Say generally that several people contributed to the resolution without naming anyone.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing publicly because the colleague did not ask to be recognized.$qb$, $qb$Allow the team to keep believing you solved it because you remained accountable for the final result.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-178$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a draft that is generally good but has the same clarity problem in several sections. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize what is working, show one or two specific examples of the recurring problem and its impact, and agree a practical way they can improve the pattern themselves.$qb$, $qb$Point out the recurring problem and suggest how you would rewrite the affected sections.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the unclear sections so the colleague can compare your version with theirs.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the draft needs to be more concise and leave them to decide what to change.$qb$, $qb$Say the draft is good overall and avoid raising the weakness because it is not serious enough to risk discouraging them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-179$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You must present a complex analysis to leaders who have little time and need to decide what to do next. The analysis contains an important caveat. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Lead with the decision-relevant finding, show the evidence and important caveat, explain why it matters, and end with the specific decision or action required.$qb$, $qb$Give a concise summary of the main findings followed by supporting charts and the caveat.$qb$, $qb$Present the analysis in the same order you performed it so the logic is transparent.$qb$, $qb$Show all available data so leaders can form their own conclusion without your interpretation.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the most dramatic finding and leave the caveat for questions because time is limited.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-180$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need input from colleagues in three time zones on a decision that is not urgent. A live meeting would fall outside normal hours for at least one person. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send concise context, the exact decision needed, relevant material, and a response deadline that gives each time zone a reasonable working window.$qb$, $qb$Send the question by email and ask everyone to reply when convenient.$qb$, $qb$Schedule the live meeting at the time that works for the largest number of participants.$qb$, $qb$Message each person separately so you can collect responses more quickly.$qb$, $qb$Mark the request urgent so everyone sees it before their local workday ends.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-189$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project lead keeps assigning note-taking and coordination work to the same junior employee while others receive the more visible analytical tasks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review how support and analytical work are allocated, rotate developmental opportunities using relevant capability and support, and share routine coordination duties more broadly.$qb$, $qb$Give the junior employee one analytical task while keeping most coordination work with them.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers for note-taking so everyone technically has the same opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Tell the junior employee to speak up if they want more visible work.$qb$, $qb$Keep the arrangement because the junior employee has become efficient at coordination.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-190$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A solution you proposed worked initially but has now created an unexpected problem for another team. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the downstream problem, involve the affected team in reviewing the cause, and change, reverse, or retest your solution based on the combined evidence.$qb$, $qb$Tell the affected team you will adjust the solution and ask them to document the impact.$qb$, $qb$Keep your solution because it solved the original problem and help the other team manage the side effect.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the downstream effect could not reasonably have been predicted.$qb$, $qb$Wait for your manager to decide whether the other team's problem is serious enough to justify change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-192$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate spots a risk in your plan early enough to prevent substantial rework. Their contribution is not obvious in the final result. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the teammate specifically and make their contribution visible when the team discusses why the rework was avoided.$qb$, $qb$Thank the teammate privately and offer to return the help later.$qb$, $qb$Mention generally that the plan benefited from team input without naming anyone.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the formal recognition cycle before mentioning the contribution.$qb$, $qb$Treat the intervention as normal peer review and say nothing.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-224$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you feedback in a blunt way, but one example they provide appears valid. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Set aside the blunt tone, ask about the example and its impact, acknowledge the valid point, and decide what change is useful while clarifying any factual misunderstanding calmly.$qb$, $qb$Accept the valid example but tell the colleague their delivery style makes feedback difficult to receive.$qb$, $qb$Thank them and change only the example they identified.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague whether they agree before deciding whether to act.$qb$, $qb$Dismiss the feedback because constructive feedback should be delivered respectfully.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-225$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project produced both a measurable benefit and an unexpected cost. Leaders ask whether it should be expanded. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Present the original problem, measurable benefit, unexpected cost, net trade-off, and your evidence-based recommendation on whether and how to expand.$qb$, $qb$Lead with the measurable benefit and include the unexpected cost in a later slide.$qb$, $qb$Present all project metrics and let leaders decide whether the benefit outweighs the cost.$qb$, $qb$Focus mainly on the unexpected cost because expansion decisions should be conservative.$qb$, $qb$Recommend expansion based only on the original success measure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-226$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A decision thread has spread across email, chat, and comments in a document. Different people now have different understandings of what was agreed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Create one durable decision record stating what was agreed, open questions, owners, and next steps; link it from the other channels and ask participants to correct any misunderstanding there.$qb$, $qb$Send a summary email and leave the existing chat and document comments as they are.$qb$, $qb$Ask everyone to reread the three channels before continuing the work.$qb$, $qb$Start a new group chat containing only the people responsible for the next steps.$qb$, $qb$Let each workstream use the interpretation it already has and reconcile differences later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-235$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team leader schedules all development sessions at a time that repeatedly excludes colleagues working an approved alternate schedule. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the participation pattern, explore rotating times or recorded and interactive alternatives, and design access so colleagues on approved schedules can participate meaningfully.$qb$, $qb$Ask the excluded colleagues to attend outside their normal schedule when a session is especially important.$qb$, $qb$Record every session so excluded colleagues can watch later.$qb$, $qb$Keep the schedule because it works for the majority of the team.$qb$, $qb$Suggest that colleagues on alternate schedules arrange their own development opportunities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-236$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a problem you criticized another team for last week was partly caused by an assumption in your own team's work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the other team what you found, acknowledge your team's contribution to the problem, correct the assumption, and revisit the joint prevention action without defending your earlier criticism.$qb$, $qb$Correct your team's assumption and tell the other team that both sides contributed.$qb$, $qb$Fix the assumption internally and avoid reopening last week's uncomfortable discussion.$qb$, $qb$Explain that your team's contribution was indirect and that the other team's error was still larger.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the other team to raise the issue again before discussing your new finding.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-238$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague spends an hour helping you understand a new process, which allows you to complete an urgent task independently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for the time and explanation, tell them how it helped the outcome, and use what you learned so you do not create repeated dependence.$qb$, $qb$Send a brief thank-you message after completing the task.$qb$, $qb$Offer to take one of their tasks in return even if you are unfamiliar with it.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager that the colleague is very helpful during your next one-to-one.$qb$, $qb$Assume they know you appreciated the help because you used their advice successfully.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-270$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You give a colleague feedback and they disagree with your interpretation, offering evidence you had not seen. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the new evidence, clarify the behavior and impact you were responding to, revise your feedback if the evidence changes it, and retain any part that remains supported.$qb$, $qb$Explain why your original observation is still valid even if there is additional context.$qb$, $qb$Withdraw the feedback completely because you did not have all the information.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide whose interpretation is correct.$qb$, $qb$End the discussion because feedback does not require agreement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-271$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service improvement reduced customer waiting time but increased staff effort. Leaders ask whether it should be extended to other teams. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Frame the original problem, quantify the customer benefit and staff cost, explain the trade-off and conditions, and recommend whether expansion should proceed or be modified.$qb$, $qb$Lead with the waiting-time improvement and mention staff effort in the detailed appendix.$qb$, $qb$Present all measures without making a recommendation so leaders can remain objective.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the staff effort because scaling a labor-intensive change is risky.$qb$, $qb$Recommend expansion because the customer metric was the original target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-272$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone sends several questions while you are offline, but the answers already exist across three different project documents. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Reply with a concise answer, link the relevant authoritative sections, and improve the shared project index or handoff so future questions can be resolved without searching multiple documents.$qb$, $qb$Send links to all three documents and let the colleague find the answers.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a live call so you can explain the information more efficiently.$qb$, $qb$Copy the relevant text into a long chat message without updating shared documentation.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the information is already documented and ask them to search more carefully.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-281$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks for volunteers for a development project, but you know some capable employees rarely volunteer for visible opportunities. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the opportunity open to volunteers but also identify eligible people against clear criteria and invite them to consider it with appropriate support.$qb$, $qb$Ask for volunteers and remind everyone that the opportunity is good for development.$qb$, $qb$Nominate the quietest employee so they receive more exposure.$qb$, $qb$Choose only from volunteers because everyone had the same chance to respond.$qb$, $qb$Give the opportunity to the most confident volunteer because they are most likely to succeed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-282$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a shortcut you introduced has saved time but also caused a small quality problem that colleagues had warned might happen. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that the concern proved valid, stop or adjust the shortcut, review the affected work, and involve the colleagues in designing a safer improvement.$qb$, $qb$Fix the affected work and modify the shortcut yourself.$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortcut because the time saving is larger than the quality issue.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the problem is minor and was an acceptable risk when the shortcut was introduced.$qb$, $qb$Wait for your manager to decide whether the shortcut should continue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-284$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague gives you candid feedback that initially feels uncomfortable but helps you avoid a mistake in an important presentation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising the issue, tell them how the feedback improved the presentation, and remain open to similarly direct input in future.$qb$, $qb$Thank them after the presentation if the change appears to work well.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you appreciated the intention even though the feedback style was uncomfortable.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because colleagues should point out mistakes as part of normal work.$qb$, $qb$Avoid asking that colleague for feedback next time because their style is difficult.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-316$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a presentation, but you have only seen the final slides and not the audience reaction or delivery. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Be clear about what you can assess from the slides, give specific feedback on those elements, and avoid making claims about delivery or audience impact you did not observe.$qb$, $qb$Give overall presentation feedback based on the slides because content is the main factor.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague how the presentation went and combine their view with yours.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague the slides look good and avoid feedback because you lack full context.$qb$, $qb$Critique the delivery based on how you imagine the slides would have been presented.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-317$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project missed its original deadline but ultimately delivered a better result after the team changed approach. Leaders ask what happened. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Explain the original objective, the evidence that triggered the approach change, the resulting delay, the improved outcome, and what should be learned for future planning.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the improved final result and mention the delay briefly.$qb$, $qb$Give a detailed chronology of every event that caused the delay.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the missed deadline because schedule performance is the clearest accountability issue.$qb$, $qb$Present only the final metrics and let leaders ask about the delay if interested.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-318$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone completes work while you are offline but repeatedly has to wait because your handovers do not state which decisions they can make independently. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Add clear decision boundaries, current status, next actions, and escalation conditions to future handovers so the colleague can progress safely without waiting for you.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague they may use their judgment whenever you are offline.$qb$, $qb$Send more detailed background information in each handover.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a daily overlap call so decisions can always be discussed live.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to leave uncertain items untouched until you return.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-327$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A training course has limited places. The proposed shortlist consists mainly of employees whose managers actively nominated them, although some teams rarely nominate anyone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use transparent eligibility criteria across the full employee pool, invite overlooked eligible people or managers to participate, and select against the same criteria rather than nomination visibility alone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the nominations but reserve one place for a team that rarely nominates people.$qb$, $qb$Ask all managers to submit more nominations and select from whoever is proposed.$qb$, $qb$Give places to employees whose managers made the strongest case.$qb$, $qb$Keep the shortlist because managers are responsible for identifying development needs.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-328$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that you dismissed a teammate's concern too quickly, and the issue they raised has now become a real problem. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that you dismissed the concern, involve the teammate in addressing the issue, and examine why you discounted the warning so you can improve future judgment.$qb$, $qb$Tell the teammate they were right and ask for their help fixing the problem.$qb$, $qb$Focus on solving the issue now and discuss the earlier disagreement only if they raise it.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the concern lacked enough evidence when it was first raised.$qb$, $qb$Let the manager handle the problem because revisiting the earlier discussion may create blame.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-330$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague takes over an urgent task for you when you have a family emergency. When you return, the work is complete and no one else knows they helped. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague personally, acknowledge their contribution to the relevant team or manager, and make sure the handover does not leave them carrying your work longer than necessary.$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately and offer to cover for them in future.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team that the task was completed through good teamwork without naming the colleague.$qb$, $qb$Buy them a gift to show your appreciation.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing publicly because the reason you needed help was personal.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-362$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback immediately after giving a presentation. You noticed one important weakness, but they are visibly pleased with how it went. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge what worked, describe the important weakness with a specific example and impact, and discuss one practical improvement while the experience is still fresh.$qb$, $qb$Congratulate them now and give the critical feedback another day.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the weakness because they already know the presentation went well.$qb$, $qb$Say it went well overall and avoid the weakness unless they ask specifically.$qb$, $qb$Send detailed written criticism later so the conversation does not affect their mood.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-363$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You need to explain why a project that met its cost target still failed to achieve the expected business benefit. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Build the story from the intended business outcome, show that cost was controlled, explain the evidence for why benefit did not materialize, and state the decision or learning that follows.$qb$, $qb$Lead with the fact that the project stayed within budget and then discuss the missing benefit.$qb$, $qb$Present the project financials and benefit metrics in separate sections without connecting them.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the failed benefit because cost performance is no longer relevant.$qb$, $qb$Describe the project activities in chronological order and let leaders draw the conclusion.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-364$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A shared document receives conflicting comments from several colleagues overnight, and nobody can tell which suggestion reflects the current decision. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Summarize the conflicting points in the document, identify the decision owner, obtain or record the decision in one place, and resolve or close obsolete comment threads.$qb$, $qb$Reply to every comment individually with your interpretation.$qb$, $qb$Accept the suggestion from the most senior commenter.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a live meeting so everyone can discuss all comments together.$qb$, $qb$Leave all comments open so the full history remains visible.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-373$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$An important meeting is always held in person, but one capable remote colleague regularly misses the informal discussion that happens immediately afterward. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Bring material post-meeting discussion into the formal shared channel, document decisions, and create an equivalent way for remote participants to contribute before decisions close.$qb$, $qb$Call the remote colleague after each meeting and tell them what was discussed.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to travel in for the most important meetings.$qb$, $qb$Continue the arrangement because the formal meeting itself is accessible remotely.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to contact attendees if they feel they missed anything.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-374$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You defend a project estimate strongly, but new information shows that your estimate was based on an assumption that is no longer valid. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State that the assumption has changed, revise the estimate transparently, explain the impact, and avoid defending the old number simply because you argued for it earlier.$qb$, $qb$Revise the estimate and send the new number without discussing why it changed.$qb$, $qb$Keep the original estimate until the new information is fully proven.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to produce the revised estimate so it appears independent.$qb$, $qb$Continue defending the original number because frequent estimate changes reduce credibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-376$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague stays late to help your team finish a task, but the next morning they make a small mistake because they are tired. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the extra support they provided, correct the mistake without using it to diminish that contribution, and consider whether the workload created an avoidable fatigue risk.$qb$, $qb$Thank them for staying late but remind them to be more careful when tired.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the mistake because accuracy remains their responsibility regardless of extra effort.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the mistake because they helped the team substantially.$qb$, $qb$Thank them publicly for staying late so the team sees the level of commitment expected.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-408$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to give feedback to a colleague whose work is technically correct but difficult for others to use. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use a specific example to show the usability impact, acknowledge the technical correctness, and agree a change that preserves accuracy while making the output easier for its users.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the work is correct but needs to be more user-friendly.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the output yourself so they can see a better version.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on technical correctness because usability is subjective.$qb$, $qb$Ask users to adapt because changing technically correct work creates unnecessary effort.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-409$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A pilot failed its headline target but revealed a customer need that could support a different product opportunity. Leaders ask whether the pilot was a failure. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$State clearly that the original target was missed, show the evidence for the newly discovered need, separate learning from success, and recommend the next decision based on that evidence.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the new opportunity so the pilot is seen as valuable despite missing the target.$qb$, $qb$Call the pilot a failure because its original success criterion was not met.$qb$, $qb$Present all pilot data without labeling the outcome.$qb$, $qb$Describe the team's effort and experimentation rather than the missed target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-410$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague posts an important decision in a fast-moving group chat, and you know several affected people are offline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move or copy the decision into the durable project record with context, owners and effective timing, then link that record in the chat so offline colleagues can find the authoritative version.$qb$, $qb$Tag the offline colleagues in the chat so they see it when they return.$qb$, $qb$Send the decision separately by email to everyone who is offline.$qb$, $qb$Leave it in chat because the platform preserves message history.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting to repeat the decision when everyone is available.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-419$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks only employees who can travel at short notice to express interest in a development assignment, although much of the assignment could be done remotely. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify which parts genuinely require travel, redesign participation where practical, and invite eligible employees based on the actual assignment requirements rather than assumed availability.$qb$, $qb$Keep the travel requirement but give remote employees a similar assignment later.$qb$, $qb$Ask remote employees to volunteer anyway and decide case by case.$qb$, $qb$Choose from employees who can travel because flexibility is useful for development roles.$qb$, $qb$Let the manager decide because they understand the assignment best.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-420$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a recommendation you made in a meeting was based on data from the wrong reporting period. No decision has been made yet. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell the group promptly, replace the incorrect evidence with the right period, reassess the recommendation, and clearly state whether your conclusion changes.$qb$, $qb$Send the corrected data without mentioning that your recommendation used the wrong period.$qb$, $qb$Wait until you know whether the corrected data changes the conclusion before saying anything.$qb$, $qb$Ask the analyst who supplied the report to explain the error to the group.$qb$, $qb$Keep the recommendation because no decision has been made and the mistake may not matter.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-422$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate gives up part of their planned work time to help you meet an urgent commitment. Their own task is now slightly delayed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically, help manage or communicate the impact on their task, and make sure their support is recognized rather than treating the delay as solely their problem.$qb$, $qb$Thank them and assume they will manage their own delayed task.$qb$, $qb$Offer to help them later if they ask.$qb$, $qb$Tell your manager you completed the urgent commitment with team support.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because they chose to help voluntarily.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-454$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a report you only partly understand because it covers a specialist area. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Be explicit about the limits of your technical expertise, give specific feedback on the parts you can judge such as clarity and logic, and suggest specialist review where technical correctness matters.$qb$, $qb$Give full feedback based on your general professional experience.$qb$, $qb$Decline to comment because you are not a specialist.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on grammar and formatting.$qb$, $qb$Ask another specialist what they think and present their feedback as your own.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-455$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer satisfaction score improved, but only after the team stopped serving a difficult customer segment. Leaders ask whether service quality improved. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the score improvement together with the change in customer mix, explain how that affects interpretation, and distinguish genuine service improvement from a changed population.$qb$, $qb$Present the improved score and mention the customer-segment change in a footnote.$qb$, $qb$Say service quality improved because the measured customers are more satisfied.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the lost difficult segment because the score is now misleading.$qb$, $qb$Present only the raw score trend and let leaders interpret it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-456$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague sends a detailed question late in your workday. Their workday is just starting, but you cannot fully answer before you leave. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send what you can confirm now, state what remains unresolved, point them to useful sources or decision boundaries, and give a clear time for the fuller answer so they can progress safely.$qb$, $qb$Stay late and provide a complete answer because their workday has just started.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you will answer tomorrow and provide no partial information.$qb$, $qb$Send a quick guess so they have something to work with.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to schedule a meeting during your next overlapping hours.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-465$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A mentoring program fills places through employee self-nomination. Participation is consistently much lower from one department whose employees say they are too busy to apply. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Investigate the workload or access barrier, keep transparent eligibility criteria, and create a nomination or participation process that gives qualified employees a realistic opportunity to join.$qb$, $qb$Reserve a fixed number of places for that department regardless of interest.$qb$, $qb$Send the department more reminders about the program.$qb$, $qb$Keep self-nomination because the process is equally open to everyone.$qb$, $qb$Ask the department manager to choose who should participate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-466$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out that a process you designed has an unnecessary step. You initially disagree, but their example shows the step adds no value in common cases. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the evidence, review when the step is genuinely needed, and simplify the process rather than defending the design because it is yours.$qb$, $qb$Remove the step from the example they showed and keep it elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Ask for several more examples before considering any change.$qb$, $qb$Explain why the step was included originally and keep the process unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Ask another person to decide so you do not appear to favor your teammate's criticism.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-468$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague challenges your approach in a meeting and their alternative saves the team several hours. Your original idea had already received positive attention. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague for the better approach, make their contribution clear to the team, and support using it without trying to preserve credit for your original idea.$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately and tell the team that the approach was refined collaboratively.$qb$, $qb$Use their approach but avoid discussing whose idea it was.$qb$, $qb$Keep your original approach because changing publicly may make the team look indecisive.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager which approach should be credited in the final report.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-500$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on work that is much better than their previous attempt but still has one important weakness. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the specific improvement, explain the remaining weakness with an example and impact, and agree the next adjustment so progress and development are both clear.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the improvement and leave the weakness for a later task.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the weakness because improvement is already obvious.$qb$, $qb$Say the work is much better and let them identify remaining issues themselves.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the weak part so the final output meets the standard.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-501$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project delivered on time and within budget, but user adoption is much lower than expected. Leaders ask for a success update. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$State the delivery achievements, show the adoption gap and its business significance, explain the evidence behind likely causes, and recommend the next action rather than labeling the project simply successful.$qb$, $qb$Lead with the on-time and on-budget delivery and mention adoption as a follow-up issue.$qb$, $qb$Focus on low adoption because business benefit matters more than delivery performance.$qb$, $qb$Present all project metrics without giving an overall interpretation.$qb$, $qb$Call the project successful because it met the formal delivery targets.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-502$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team member updates a shared document overnight but does not explain what changed. Several colleagues begin reviewing the old assumptions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for or add a concise change note stating what changed and why, notify affected reviewers, and establish a lightweight change-summary practice for material updates.$qb$, $qb$Tell reviewers to use version history to identify the changes themselves.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team member to send a separate email every time they edit the document.$qb$, $qb$Restore the previous version until everyone has reviewed the new one.$qb$, $qb$Leave the document as it is because shared tools already record every edit.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-511$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A recurring client meeting is scheduled at a time that works for senior team members but repeatedly conflicts with an approved caregiving schedule for one capable colleague. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review whether the meeting time can rotate or shift without harming the client, and create a participation method that gives the colleague meaningful access rather than permanent exclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to join only the most important meetings outside their normal schedule.$qb$, $qb$Send them the minutes after every meeting.$qb$, $qb$Keep the time because senior and client availability should take priority.$qb$, $qb$Replace the colleague on the account with someone who can attend consistently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-512$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn that a teammate quietly corrected an error in a calculation you had approved. The final result is right, but you had told others the calculation was already checked. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the teammate, acknowledge that your approval missed the error, correct any inaccurate impression about the review, and improve the checking method that allowed it through.$qb$, $qb$Thank the teammate privately and leave the final result unchanged because it is now correct.$qb$, $qb$Ask why the teammate changed an approved calculation without consulting you.$qb$, $qb$Say the team review process worked because someone eventually caught the error.$qb$, $qb$Avoid mentioning it because the final result is accurate and no harm occurred.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-514$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague shares a reusable template they created, saving you significant preparation time. They say it was 'nothing special.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for the useful template, explain the time or quality benefit it created, and credit them if the template is shared more widely.$qb$, $qb$Thank them briefly and start using the template.$qb$, $qb$Offer to create a template for them in return.$qb$, $qb$Share the template with others without naming the creator because they said it was nothing special.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing further because they clearly do not want recognition.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-546$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback but says they have only five minutes before another meeting. You have several points, one of which is important. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use the time for the most important specific point and its impact, acknowledge what worked, and arrange a later discussion for lower-priority feedback if useful.$qb$, $qb$Give all your feedback quickly so nothing is missed.$qb$, $qb$Give only positive feedback because there is not enough time for a difficult discussion.$qb$, $qb$Tell them feedback needs more time and give none.$qb$, $qb$Send all critical points by chat while they are in the next meeting.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-547$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A cost-saving project met its savings target but created longer customer wait times. Leaders ask whether the project should continue. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the intended saving, actual financial result, customer wait-time impact, total trade-off, and options for retaining savings while correcting the service cost.$qb$, $qb$Lead with the achieved savings and mention wait time as an operational issue.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the wait-time increase because customer impact outweighs cost savings.$qb$, $qb$Present both metrics without recommending what they mean.$qb$, $qb$Call the project successful because it achieved the approved financial target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-548$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague sends you a document for approval while you are offline, but the message does not say what changed from the version you previously reviewed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask for or create a concise change summary, review the changed sections and any dependent content, and establish that future approval requests identify material changes.$qb$, $qb$Review the entire document again from the beginning.$qb$, $qb$Approve it if the document title and date are newer.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to wait until you can discuss the changes live.$qb$, $qb$Reject the document because the colleague did not provide a change summary.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-557$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A development assignment requires occasional evening calls with another country. A capable employee with caregiving responsibilities is interested but unsure whether they can participate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Clarify how many evening calls are genuinely necessary, explore rotation or scheduling alternatives, and assess the employee against the assignment requirements rather than assuming their caregiving makes participation impractical.$qb$, $qb$Tell the employee the evening calls are part of the opportunity and let them decide whether to apply.$qb$, $qb$Offer the assignment to someone with fewer outside commitments.$qb$, $qb$Give the employee a smaller internal development task instead.$qb$, $qb$Remove evening calls entirely so the employee can participate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-558$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You realize that a team rule you argued strongly for is creating more administration than value. No one has complained directly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the evidence yourself, acknowledge that the rule is not producing the intended value, and propose removing or redesigning it rather than waiting for others to challenge you.$qb$, $qb$Quietly simplify the rule for your own work first.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the team to raise concerns so you know the burden is significant.$qb$, $qb$Keep the rule because changing it after advocating for it may reduce confidence.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to review the rule without mentioning your own concern.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-560$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate takes time to review your work and finds no errors, but their questions help you explain the reasoning more clearly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for the thoughtful review, explain how their questions improved the clarity, and recognize that useful review can add value even when it finds no defect.$qb$, $qb$Thank them briefly for checking the work.$qb$, $qb$Say the review was unnecessary because the work was already correct.$qb$, $qb$Offer to review their next task in return.$qb$, $qb$Do not mention it because reviewers are expected to ask questions.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-592$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a decision that turned out well, but you think their reasoning involved unnecessary risk. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the good outcome, separate outcome from decision quality, discuss the risk in their reasoning with a concrete example, and explore a safer approach for similar future decisions.$qb$, $qb$Say the decision worked but they were lucky.$qb$, $qb$Give only positive feedback because the result was good.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to use a safer method next time without discussing the reasoning.$qb$, $qb$Do not give feedback because criticizing a successful decision may confuse them.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-593$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project produced no immediate financial return but removed a major operational risk. Leaders ask what value it delivered. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Explain the original risk, its likelihood and impact, how the project changed exposure, the cost of mitigation, and the resulting business protection without pretending avoided loss is realized revenue.$qb$, $qb$Convert the avoided risk into an estimated financial saving and present that as project return.$qb$, $qb$Say the project had no financial return but was necessary for compliance.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the project activities completed successfully.$qb$, $qb$Present only the project cost because risk reduction cannot be measured reliably.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-594$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A decision needs approval from a colleague who will be offline for most of your workday. You know the questions they usually ask. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Send the decision request with concise context, options, recommendation, likely questions and a clear response deadline so they can review it independently when online.$qb$, $qb$Wait until your working hours overlap and discuss it live.$qb$, $qb$Send only the recommended option so the colleague has less to read.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone in their time zone to obtain the approval for you.$qb$, $qb$Mark the message urgent without adding more context.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-603$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team is selecting someone to attend a client workshop. The preferred employee is highly capable but has already received several similar opportunities this year. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess all eligible employees against the workshop requirements, consider who can succeed with reasonable preparation, and avoid treating prior opportunity as the main qualification for receiving more opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Send the preferred employee because the client workshop is important.$qb$, $qb$Choose someone who has had no opportunities this year regardless of readiness.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to vote on who deserves the workshop most.$qb$, $qb$Give the preferred employee the workshop but promise the next one to someone else.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-604$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a teammate's approach you opposed is producing better results than the method you recommended. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the better evidence, support the teammate's approach, and examine what assumption in your own recommendation was wrong so the learning can be reused.$qb$, $qb$Agree to use the teammate's approach without discussing the earlier disagreement.$qb$, $qb$Ask for more time before changing because the result may be temporary.$qb$, $qb$Combine both approaches so neither recommendation is fully rejected.$qb$, $qb$Keep your method because consistency matters more than short-term results.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-606$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague shares a mistake they made so your team can avoid repeating it. The disclosure is uncomfortable for them but useful to everyone. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for sharing the learning, focus discussion on what the team can change, and avoid using their openness as a reason to embarrass or blame them.$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately after the meeting.$qb$, $qb$Tell the team that everyone makes mistakes and move on quickly.$qb$, $qb$Ask detailed questions about exactly why they made the mistake before discussing prevention.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because admitting mistakes is part of professional responsibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-638$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks whether their report is ready to send. It is strong overall, but one conclusion is not supported by the evidence shown. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them the report is close, point to the unsupported conclusion and the evidence gap, and ask them to revise or qualify that conclusion before sending.$qb$, $qb$Say the report is good but suggest they double-check the conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the conclusion yourself so the report can go out quickly.$qb$, $qb$Approve the report because the unsupported point is only one part of it.$qb$, $qb$Reject the whole report without explaining which part is weak.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-639$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team improved processing speed but customer satisfaction did not change. Leaders expected faster processing to improve satisfaction. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the speed improvement, the unchanged satisfaction result, examine whether speed was actually a customer pain point, and explain what the evidence suggests should be tested next.$qb$, $qb$Present the speed improvement as a successful operational outcome.$qb$, $qb$Say the project failed because customer satisfaction did not improve.$qb$, $qb$Present both metrics without explaining the relationship.$qb$, $qb$Argue that satisfaction will probably improve later if speed remains high.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-640$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks a question that depends on a decision still being discussed in your local team. They will be offline before the decision is final. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Tell them what is currently known, identify the pending decision and likely options, give any safe work they can progress, and record the final decision where they can find it when they return.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the decision is final and reply the next day.$qb$, $qb$Give them the most likely decision so they can keep working.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to stay online later until the local discussion ends.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to pause all related work until you can speak live.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-649$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager tends to choose employees who speak most confidently in meetings for client-facing opportunities. Some quieter employees have equally strong work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Define the capabilities the client work requires, consider evidence from actual performance as well as meeting style, and provide preparation support where a capable employee needs more exposure.$qb$, $qb$Choose the confident speakers because client work requires confidence.$qb$, $qb$Rotate client opportunities equally regardless of performance.$qb$, $qb$Ask quieter employees to volunteer if they want client exposure.$qb$, $qb$Keep the manager's approach because clients respond well to confident communication.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-650$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a colleague's criticism of your plan was correct, but you had dismissed it publicly as overly cautious. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge publicly that the concern was valid, adjust the plan, and examine why you dismissed the evidence so quickly rather than minimizing the reversal.$qb$, $qb$Change the plan and thank the colleague privately.$qb$, $qb$Say new information has changed the plan without revisiting the earlier criticism.$qb$, $qb$Keep part of your original plan so the change does not appear too large.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the colleague to raise the concern again before changing course.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-652$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague introduces you to someone whose advice helps solve a difficult problem. The colleague did not solve the problem directly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague for making the useful connection and explain how it contributed to the solution, recognizing enabling help as well as direct technical work.$qb$, $qb$Thank only the person who gave the technical advice.$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague briefly but do not mention the connection when discussing the solution.$qb$, $qb$Offer the colleague a favor in return.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because introductions are a normal part of teamwork.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-684$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a draft that uses a different style from yours but communicates the message clearly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Judge it against the intended audience and outcome, recognize what works, and suggest changes only where the style creates a real clarity, risk, or effectiveness issue.$qb$, $qb$Recommend changing it to the style you normally use because consistency helps quality.$qb$, $qb$Say it is fine and give no feedback because style is subjective.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite a section in your style so they can compare.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager which style the team should standardize on.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-685$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project exceeded its budget but prevented a larger operational loss after an unexpected risk emerged. Leaders ask whether the overspend was justified. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Explain the original budget, the emerging risk, the decision that increased cost, the loss avoided or exposure reduced with appropriate uncertainty, and the trade-off leaders should learn from.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the avoided loss so the overspend appears reasonable.$qb$, $qb$Focus on the budget variance because financial control was missed.$qb$, $qb$Present the timeline of extra spending without interpreting it.$qb$, $qb$Call the project successful because the operational loss did not occur.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-686$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague updates a shared plan while you are offline and leaves several unresolved questions inside comments. Your work depends on two of them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Respond in the shared plan with the decisions or information you can provide, identify the two blocking questions clearly, and keep the resolution in the same durable context rather than starting a separate private thread.$qb$, $qb$Send the colleague a chat message listing all your answers.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting to discuss every comment.$qb$, $qb$Make assumptions for the two blocking questions and continue.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the colleague is online again before doing anything.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-695$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-profile assignment requires presentation skills. A capable employee has little presentation experience because they have rarely been given such opportunities. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess whether they can meet the assignment with reasonable preparation, offer rehearsal or support, and avoid using lack of prior opportunity as automatic evidence of lack of capability.$qb$, $qb$Choose someone with proven presentation experience because the assignment is high profile.$qb$, $qb$Give the employee the assignment regardless of readiness so they finally receive exposure.$qb$, $qb$Let employees vote on who presents best.$qb$, $qb$Ask the employee to gain presentation experience on lower-profile work first.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-696$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A decision you supported turns out poorly even though the information available at the time seemed reasonable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the decision process honestly, separate unforeseeable outcome from any avoidable reasoning weakness, own your part, and capture what should change in future decisions.$qb$, $qb$Explain that the decision was reasonable with the information available and move on.$qb$, $qb$Take full blame because you supported the decision.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the new information that could not have been known.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone who opposed the decision to lead the review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-698$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague notices you are overloaded and proactively takes a routine task without being asked. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for noticing and helping, confirm the handoff and impact, and make sure their initiative does not quietly create overload or unclear ownership for them.$qb$, $qb$Thank them and let them keep the task permanently.$qb$, $qb$Tell them they should have asked before taking work from you.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the task was routine and easy.$qb$, $qb$Publicly praise them for working beyond their responsibilities.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-730$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate asks what they should learn from a successful decision that you considered risky at the time. How do you respond?$qb$, $qb$Review the assumptions they used, identify which were well judged and which could have produced a poor result in another situation, and discuss how to preserve the good reasoning while controlling the risk next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the success shows their judgment was better than you expected.$qb$, $qb$Explain the risk you saw and advise them not to use the same approach again.$qb$, $qb$Congratulate them and avoid revisiting the disagreement because the outcome was good.$qb$, $qb$Say the outcome was mostly luck and recommend your original approach for future cases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-731$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A transformation project delivered fewer features than planned but users adopted the delivered features much more strongly than expected. Leaders ask whether the project succeeded. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Compare planned scope, actual delivery, adoption and business outcomes, explain the trade-off, and judge success against value rather than either feature count or adoption alone.$qb$, $qb$Call it successful because adoption exceeded expectations.$qb$, $qb$Call it unsuccessful because planned scope was missed.$qb$, $qb$Present the feature and adoption numbers without an overall conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Focus on why the missing features were delayed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-732$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague leaves a long voice message with several decisions and requests while you are offline. Other team members need the same information. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Convert the material decisions, requests and owners into the shared written record, clarify anything ambiguous with the sender, and link the team to that durable summary.$qb$, $qb$Forward the voice message to everyone who needs it.$qb$, $qb$Reply to the colleague privately and tell others what matters later.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to record a shorter message.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting so the information can be repeated live.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-741$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager proposes giving a stretch assignment only to employees rated highest last year. One capable employee was on extended leave during part of that rating period. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use current role-relevant evidence and assignment requirements, consider whether the prior rating is comparable, and avoid allowing an unusual absence period to become an automatic barrier.$qb$, $qb$Use last year's ratings because they are the formal performance record.$qb$, $qb$Give the employee on leave the assignment to compensate for the missed period.$qb$, $qb$Exclude anyone without a full year of performance evidence.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to choose based on personal knowledge of the employees.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-742$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You approve a plan that later needs rework because you overlooked a dependency mentioned in the supporting notes. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that your review missed the dependency, help recover the plan, and change your review approach so critical dependencies are checked explicitly next time.$qb$, $qb$Help fix the plan and avoid focusing on who missed the dependency.$qb$, $qb$Point out that the plan author should have highlighted the dependency more clearly.$qb$, $qb$Ask another reviewer to check future plans instead of you.$qb$, $qb$Treat the rework as normal because complex plans often change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-744$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate takes time to explain why your proposed shortcut could create a future problem. You change the plan and the risk is avoided. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for challenging the shortcut and explain how their reasoning improved the plan, reinforcing that constructive challenge is valued.$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately after the work is complete.$qb$, $qb$Say the team made a good decision without identifying their contribution.$qb$, $qb$Do not emphasize the challenge because it may make your original idea look weak.$qb$, $qb$Offer to support one of their ideas in return.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-776$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks whether their analysis is good. The analysis is sound, but the recommendation is too confident for the evidence. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the sound analysis, show where the recommendation goes beyond the evidence, and help them calibrate the wording or decision to the actual confidence level.$qb$, $qb$Say the analysis is good and suggest making the recommendation slightly softer.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the recommendation for them.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the underlying analysis is correct.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the whole analysis is weak because the conclusion is overstated.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-777$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project reduced processing cost but increased the number of customers who needed follow-up help. Leaders ask whether efficiency improved. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the direct cost saving, added follow-up workload and customer impact, calculate the net operational effect where possible, and explain whether the apparent efficiency survives end-to-end analysis.$qb$, $qb$Lead with the processing cost reduction because that was the project goal.$qb$, $qb$Focus on increased follow-up because customer effort matters most.$qb$, $qb$Present both metrics without combining them.$qb$, $qb$Call the project efficient because the original process is cheaper.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-778$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks you to review a decision document. You can review it now, but one question requires input from someone who is offline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Complete the parts you can review, mark the one unresolved dependency clearly, provide any conditional guidance, and keep the document moving instead of blocking the entire review.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the other person is online and review everything together.$qb$, $qb$Approve the document and assume the missing input will not change it.$qb$, $qb$Send the unresolved question by chat and stop reviewing.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting across all three time zones.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-787$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team asks employees to nominate themselves for a visible assignment. You notice people new to the organization rarely nominate themselves even when qualified. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep transparent criteria, actively inform all eligible employees what the assignment involves, and invite qualified people to consider it rather than relying only on confidence in self-nomination.$qb$, $qb$Reserve places for the newest employees.$qb$, $qb$Keep self-nomination because everyone receives the same message.$qb$, $qb$Ask managers to nominate people instead.$qb$, $qb$Choose only people who volunteer because motivation matters.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-788$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague finds that a calculation method you recommended creates a small bias in some cases. The overall results you produced remain usable. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the limitation, assess which past results are affected, correct the method, and communicate any material impact without defending it because most results remain usable.$qb$, $qb$Fix the method for future work and leave past results alone.$qb$, $qb$Ask for more examples before accepting that the method is biased.$qb$, $qb$Keep the method because the bias is small.$qb$, $qb$Let the colleague own the correction because they found the issue.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-790$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague corrects you privately before you present an inaccurate point to a large group. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for intervening early, correct your material, and recognize that their private challenge protected both the outcome and your credibility.$qb$, $qb$Thank them after the presentation if nobody notices the change.$qb$, $qb$Avoid mentioning it because the correction could be embarrassing.$qb$, $qb$Tell them you would have caught the issue yourself during the presentation.$qb$, $qb$Offer to review their next presentation in return.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-822$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a proposal you disagree with, but their reasoning is well structured and evidence-based. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the quality of the reasoning, identify the specific assumption or trade-off where your view differs, and discuss that disagreement without presenting your preference as a defect.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the proposal is well written but recommend your preferred option.$qb$, $qb$Give only positive feedback because their reasoning is sound.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the recommendation to match your conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask a third person to decide which proposal is better before giving feedback.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-823$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A program met every activity milestone but the expected customer behavior did not change. Leaders ask for a completion story. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show that execution milestones were met, contrast them with the unchanged customer outcome, examine the assumed link between activity and behavior, and recommend what should change next.$qb$, $qb$Present the milestone completion as evidence that the program was delivered successfully.$qb$, $qb$Call the program a failure because behavior did not change.$qb$, $qb$List activities and customer metrics separately without connecting them.$qb$, $qb$Argue that customer behavior may change later.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-824$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for approval in a chat message, but the decision will matter months later and the project record contains no context. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the request and rationale into the durable decision record, make the approval there, and link it in chat so future users can understand what was decided and why.$qb$, $qb$Approve in chat and take a screenshot for the project folder.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to email you instead.$qb$, $qb$Approve now and document it only if someone asks later.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting so the decision is formally discussed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-833$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A client visit has limited places. The same employees are usually chosen because they already know the client well. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Identify which roles genuinely require existing client knowledge, then use remaining places to develop other capable employees with preparation and clear responsibilities.$qb$, $qb$Keep the usual employees because client familiarity reduces risk.$qb$, $qb$Rotate all places regardless of client knowledge.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees who have never attended to volunteer.$qb$, $qb$Give one new employee a place but no client-facing responsibility.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-834$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You learn that an efficiency estimate you presented was too optimistic because you overlooked a recurring exception. Leaders have not yet acted on it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the estimate before a decision is made, explain the overlooked exception and its effect, and update your estimation method to include such cases.$qb$, $qb$Quietly update the number in the next version of the presentation.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether leaders use the estimate before correcting it.$qb$, $qb$Explain that exceptions are difficult to predict and keep the original estimate.$qb$, $qb$Ask the process owner to correct the estimate because they know the exception better.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-836$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate covers a customer call for you and also leaves clear notes that save you time when you return. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for both covering the call and creating a useful handover, and recognize the quality of the support rather than only the visible customer interaction.$qb$, $qb$Thank them for taking the call.$qb$, $qb$Offer to cover their next call in return.$qb$, $qb$Use the notes without mentioning them because good handovers are expected.$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager the teammate is dependable without thanking them directly.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-868$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a solution that works, but you think it is unnecessarily difficult for others to maintain. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that it works, show the maintenance burden with a concrete example, and discuss whether a simpler design can preserve the result.$qb$, $qb$Tell them the solution is too complicated and should be simplified.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite it yourself in a simpler way.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because functional correctness matters most.$qb$, $qb$Reject it without acknowledging that it works.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-869$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A service change reduced average handling time but increased variation: simple cases are much faster while complex cases are slower. Leaders ask whether performance improved. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the average change and the split by case complexity, explain who benefited and who worsened, and assess whether the trade-off matches service goals.$qb$, $qb$Say performance improved because average handling time fell.$qb$, $qb$Say performance worsened because complex cases slowed.$qb$, $qb$Present only the case-level table without a conclusion.$qb$, $qb$Average the simple and complex improvements again using equal weights.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-870$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague makes an important decision in a document comment and resolves the comment immediately, making the decision hard to find later. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the decision and rationale into the durable decision log or document body, link it to the relevant section, and use comments for discussion rather than the only record of material decisions.$qb$, $qb$Reopen the comment so it remains visible.$qb$, $qb$Take a screenshot of the resolved comment.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to email the decision to everyone.$qb$, $qb$Leave it because document history preserves resolved comments.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-879$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team plans an optional networking event after normal hours, but important relationships and opportunities often develop there. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Provide meaningful networking or visibility routes within normal work as well, and avoid treating evening attendance as evidence of commitment.$qb$, $qb$Keep the event because attendance is optional.$qb$, $qb$Move every social event into working hours.$qb$, $qb$Tell absent employees to network separately.$qb$, $qb$Record the event for people who cannot attend.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-880$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate shows that a metric you chose encourages behavior you did not intend, although the dashboard result looks good. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the unintended incentive, examine the real outcome, and change or balance the metric rather than defending it because reported performance looks strong.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric but tell people not to game it.$qb$, $qb$Add a note explaining the limitation.$qb$, $qb$Wait for stronger evidence of harm.$qb$, $qb$Ask another team to choose a replacement metric.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-882$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague introduces you to a process owner who resolves an issue your team had struggled with for days. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague for making the connection and explain how it unlocked the solution, recognizing relationship-building as a valuable contribution.$qb$, $qb$Thank only the process owner.$qb$, $qb$Thank the colleague privately but give the process owner all formal credit.$qb$, $qb$Offer a useful introduction in return.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because connecting people is normal teamwork.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-925$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$Training places mostly go to one manager's staff because that manager nominates actively.$qb$, $qb$Apply common eligibility criteria across the wider qualified pool and invite overlooked candidates before selection.$qb$, $qb$Accept the nominations.$qb$, $qb$Give every department equal places.$qb$, $qb$Reserve places for people never trained.$qb$, $qb$Choose the first nominations.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-926$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You rejected an idea, but a small controlled trial later shows it works better than expected.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the evidence, revisit the assumptions behind your rejection, and support broader use if the evidence remains sound.$qb$, $qb$Let the trial continue but keep your original view.$qb$, $qb$Say the trial is too small to matter.$qb$, $qb$Support it without discussing your earlier view.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone neutral to decide.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-928$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate catches a scheduling conflict in your plan before it affects anyone.$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically, correct the plan, and recognize preventive contribution even though no visible failure occurred.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only if delay was certain.$qb$, $qb$Say you would have noticed later.$qb$, $qb$Offer to check their schedule in return.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because review is expected.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-960$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on an automation that works but only its creator can maintain.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that it works, show the support risk, and discuss documentation, simplification or knowledge transfer needed for maintainability.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because it works.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite it yourself.$qb$, $qb$Reject automation entirely.$qb$, $qb$Tell the creator to remain the permanent owner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-961$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new support channel reduced telephone calls but increased unresolved chat conversations. Leaders ask whether service demand improved. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Present the overall improvement and the segment deterioration, quantify materiality, and explain whether targeted action is needed.$qb$, $qb$Say complaints improved overall.$qb$, $qb$Focus only on the high-value group.$qb$, $qb$Average the two trends.$qb$, $qb$List the numbers without interpretation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-962$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague changes a delivery date in a private message to one teammate, while the shared schedule still shows the old date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Move the decision and rationale into the shared authoritative record, alert affected people, and assess any work already started from the old plan.$qb$, $qb$Forward the private chat.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the next meeting.$qb$, $qb$Let affected people discover the change.$qb$, $qb$Ask the decision-makers to repeat it live.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-971$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project has one workshop requiring travel, but the remaining assignment can be done normally. A capable employee cannot travel on that date. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess whether the workshop role can be covered or adapted while the employee performs the rest of the assignment rather than excluding them entirely.$qb$, $qb$Exclude them because travel is in the plan.$qb$, $qb$Move the whole workshop.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the workshop.$qb$, $qb$Choose only employees with unrestricted travel.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-972$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You confidently said a customer would not need a feature, but new customer evidence shows they do. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the incorrect assumption, update the plan, and review why you were confident without enough validation.$qb$, $qb$Quietly add the feature.$qb$, $qb$Say the customer changed their mind.$qb$, $qb$Wait for more evidence.$qb$, $qb$Ask someone else to own the correction.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-974$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate creates an unusually clear handover before leave, preventing several likely questions. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for the quality of the handover and recognize how it reduced dependency and risk.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only if someone uses it.$qb$, $qb$Say handovers are expected.$qb$, $qb$Offer to do their work while away.$qb$, $qb$Praise the hours spent preparing it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1006$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A technically successful automation can currently be maintained only by its creator.$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge that it works, identify the support risk, and agree documentation, simplification or knowledge transfer before relying on it broadly.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because it works.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite it yourself.$qb$, $qb$Reject automation entirely.$qb$, $qb$Keep the creator as permanent sole owner.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2810$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager wants to nominate only employees who can attend an evening networking dinner for a development program, although the program itself runs during work hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate the optional dinner from the actual program requirements and assess candidates on relevant capability and availability for the program itself.$qb$, $qb$Accept the manager's approach because networking is valuable.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the dinner so everyone has identical access.$qb$, $qb$Reserve half the places for people who cannot attend evenings.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to choose between the dinner and the program.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2811$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You strongly supported a workflow change. After two weeks, data shows it saves time for your team but creates more work for another team. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the wider impact, review the end-to-end result with the other team, and revise the workflow if total value is worse than expected.$qb$, $qb$Keep the change because your team's efficiency improved.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to optimize its part of the process.$qb$, $qb$Wait another month before discussing the impact.$qb$, $qb$Reverse the change immediately without understanding the new workload.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2812$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices a small error in your customer presentation and fixes it before the customer sees it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for catching it, verify the corrected material, and recognize their preventive contribution rather than treating it as insignificant because no customer impact occurred.$qb$, $qb$Thank them privately but do not mention the contribution again.$qb$, $qb$Say the error was minor and would probably not have mattered.$qb$, $qb$Offer to check their next presentation in return.$qb$, $qb$Avoid discussing it because admitting the error may reduce confidence.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2835$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a presentation that is clear and accurate but contains twice as many slides as the audience needs. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the clarity and accuracy, show where excess detail weakens the audience experience, and help them prioritize the slides needed for the decision.$qb$, $qb$Tell them simply to cut the deck in half.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the content is accurate.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the deck yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the presentation is poor because it is too long.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2836$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project met its deadline but required substantial emergency overtime that was not in the plan. Leaders ask whether delivery was successful. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the on-time outcome together with the unplanned effort, explain what caused the capacity gap, and distinguish delivery success from sustainability and planning quality.$qb$, $qb$Call it successful because the deadline was met.$qb$, $qb$Call it unsuccessful because overtime was used.$qb$, $qb$Present only the overtime cost.$qb$, $qb$Avoid mentioning overtime because it was an internal matter.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2837$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks a complex question. You can answer most of it now, but one part depends on a decision that will be made after they go offline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Answer the confirmed parts, clearly isolate the pending decision, give any safe conditional guidance, and record the final answer where they can find it later.$qb$, $qb$Wait until every part is known.$qb$, $qb$Give your best guess on the pending decision.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to stay online longer.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting for the next overlap and provide nothing now.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1109$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A high-visibility assignment is normally given to employees who can travel at short notice. This time, all key meetings are already scheduled and no extra travel is expected. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess candidates against the actual assignment requirements and capability, rather than using unrestricted travel flexibility as a historical shortcut for selection.$qb$, $qb$Choose the usual highly mobile employees because plans can always change.$qb$, $qb$Give the assignment only to someone who has never had a high-visibility opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to promise they can travel at short notice anyway.$qb$, $qb$Let the previous assignment holder choose their successor.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1110$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You told the team that a new template would reduce rework. After a month, rework has not changed and people say the template adds effort. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the evidence and user experience, acknowledge that the expected benefit has not appeared, and change or remove the template unless a valid benefit justifies it.$qb$, $qb$Keep the template longer because one month may be too soon.$qb$, $qb$Tell people to use the template more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Remove it immediately without checking whether adoption was correct.$qb$, $qb$Ask another team to decide whether your template works.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1112$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices that your meeting agenda misses an important dependency and tells you before invitations are sent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for catching it early, update the agenda and relevant attendees, and recognize that their intervention prevented a coordination problem.$qb$, $qb$Add the dependency but say nothing because agendas are drafts.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only if the dependency later proves important.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to rewrite the whole agenda.$qb$, $qb$Keep the agenda unchanged to avoid adding more attendees.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1144$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a concise report. It is easy to read, but one important limitation is omitted because they wanted to keep it short. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the clarity, explain why the limitation is decision-relevant, and help include it briefly without turning the report into unnecessary detail.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because concise reports should avoid caveats.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to add every limitation they can think of.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the report yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the report is misleading and give no positive feedback.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1145$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project delivered the planned output, but users are creating manual workarounds because one part of the solution is difficult to use. Leaders ask whether implementation is complete. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the delivered output together with the workaround behavior and its impact, distinguish technical delivery from effective adoption, and recommend the usability action needed.$qb$, $qb$Say implementation is complete because the solution was delivered.$qb$, $qb$Say implementation failed because any workaround means failure.$qb$, $qb$Present only the number of workarounds.$qb$, $qb$Wait until users formally complain before mentioning adoption.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1146$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks you to approve a plan. Most of it is clear, but one assumption needs confirmation from someone who will not be online for six hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review and comment on everything else now, clearly mark the one assumption preventing final approval, and leave a precise request so the plan can close when the evidence arrives.$qb$, $qb$Wait six hours before reviewing any part of the plan.$qb$, $qb$Approve it and assume the missing assumption is correct.$qb$, $qb$Reject the whole plan because one assumption is unresolved.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting for the next overlap instead of progressing asynchronously.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1155$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager suggests excluding a capable employee from a customer workshop because the employee speaks with a strong regional accent. Customers have not reported difficulty understanding them. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess communication based on actual clarity and workshop requirements, provide normal preparation if useful, and do not treat accent alone as evidence of inability.$qb$, $qb$Choose someone with a more familiar accent to reduce any risk.$qb$, $qb$Give the employee the workshop automatically to prove fairness.$qb$, $qb$Ask customers in advance whether they are comfortable with the accent.$qb$, $qb$Move the employee to non-speaking work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1156$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process you designed reduces errors but takes much longer than expected, creating a backlog. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Own the trade-off, measure error reduction against delay and workload, and redesign the process to preserve necessary controls without defending the original design.$qb$, $qb$Keep the process because fewer errors are the main goal.$qb$, $qb$Remove the control that takes the most time without analysis.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to work faster with the existing process.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the backlog becomes critical before changing anything.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1158$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate stays late to help you finish an urgent task, but the task became urgent partly because you started it late. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically for the help, acknowledge your own contribution to the urgency, and improve your planning rather than praising emergency effort as the normal solution.$qb$, $qb$Thank them publicly for always going the extra mile.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because the urgency was partly your fault.$qb$, $qb$Offer to stay late for them next time.$qb$, $qb$Tell the manager the teammate deserves recognition for overtime.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1190$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a dashboard that looks polished but makes the most important risk difficult to notice. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the clear presentation elements, show how the visual hierarchy hides the material risk, and suggest a layout that makes decision-critical information easier to see.$qb$, $qb$Say the dashboard looks good and leave the risk explanation to meetings.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to make the risk red and larger without discussing the overall hierarchy.$qb$, $qb$Redesign the dashboard yourself.$qb$, $qb$Reject the dashboard because appearance should never matter.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1191$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project achieved its cost target by reducing support after launch, but users now wait longer for help. Leaders ask whether the project delivered its benefits. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the cost saving together with the service impact and downstream consequences, then assess whether the net benefit still matches the intended outcome.$qb$, $qb$Say the benefit was delivered because the cost target was met.$qb$, $qb$Say the project failed because support worsened.$qb$, $qb$Present the cost and wait-time numbers separately without interpretation.$qb$, $qb$Exclude support performance because it was outside project scope.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1192$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone leaves several questions in a shared document. You can answer three now, but the fourth needs a local expert who returns tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Answer the three confirmed questions in the document, clearly mark the fourth dependency and owner, and let the colleague progress anything that does not depend on it.$qb$, $qb$Wait until all four answers are available.$qb$, $qb$Guess the fourth answer so they can continue.$qb$, $qb$Send the three answers in a private message.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting for tomorrow instead of answering now.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1201$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stretch assignment requires occasional presentations. A capable employee has a speech impairment and asks whether presentations can be shared with a co-presenter. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess the actual assignment outcomes, consider a reasonable co-presenting arrangement that preserves meaningful participation, and evaluate the employee on relevant capability rather than one delivery format.$qb$, $qb$Exclude them because independent presentation is part of development.$qb$, $qb$Remove all presentations from the assignment.$qb$, $qb$Give them the assignment but have someone else do every presentation.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team whether co-presenting would be fair.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1202$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You argued that a recurring meeting was essential. After three months, attendance and decision data show almost no value. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Raise the evidence yourself, acknowledge that the meeting is not serving its intended purpose, and redesign or stop it while preserving any genuinely needed coordination.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because stopping it may look inconsistent.$qb$, $qb$Shorten it slightly without questioning whether it is needed.$qb$, $qb$Wait for others to complain.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager to decide without stating your own view.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1204$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague quietly fixes a formula in your report before distribution and explains the error so you can avoid it next time. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for both preventing the error and explaining the cause, correct your method, and recognize their constructive support rather than only the final fix.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only for fixing the formula.$qb$, $qb$Say the error was too small to matter.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to check all your future reports.$qb$, $qb$Offer to fix one of their reports in return.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1236$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a process note that is accurate but uses technical terms its intended users may not understand.$qb$, $qb$Recognize the accuracy, identify terms likely to block the audience, and help replace or explain them without oversimplifying the required meaning.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because technical accuracy is most important.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to remove every technical term.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the note yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the note is poor because users may find it difficult.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1237$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project increased online self-service and reduced support staffing, but customers with complex cases now wait much longer.$qb$, $qb$Present the self-service benefit, staffing change and complex-case service impact together, and assess whether the net customer and cost outcome matches the intended strategy.$qb$, $qb$Call it successful because self-service increased.$qb$, $qb$Call it unsuccessful because complex customers wait longer.$qb$, $qb$Present only total support cost.$qb$, $qb$Exclude complex cases because they are a minority.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1238$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone updates a shared draft while you are asleep, but their comments do not say which changes need your decision.$qb$, $qb$Respond in the shared draft by separating decisions from informational comments, answer what you can, and establish clearer decision tagging for future asynchronous reviews.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting to discuss every comment.$qb$, $qb$Reply privately asking what they need.$qb$, $qb$Ignore comments that are not clearly questions.$qb$, $qb$Accept all changes to avoid delay.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1247$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team selects presenters based on who can speak without notes. A capable employee communicates clearly but uses brief notes to stay organized. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess presenters on clarity, accuracy and audience engagement, and do not treat appropriate use of notes as a disqualifier unless memorized delivery is genuinely required.$qb$, $qb$Choose people who speak without notes because they appear more confident.$qb$, $qb$Give the employee the presentation automatically to broaden opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Ask the employee to memorize the presentation before being considered.$qb$, $qb$Use only senior employees for important presentations.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1248$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You recommended a new approval step to prevent errors. Errors fell, but the step now delays low-risk work significantly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review which errors the step prevents, segment the risk, and redesign the control so stronger approval is retained where needed without imposing the same delay on low-risk work.$qb$, $qb$Keep the step because errors fell.$qb$, $qb$Remove the approval step completely.$qb$, $qb$Tell low-risk teams to plan around the delay.$qb$, $qb$Wait until someone formally requests a change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1250$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate takes over a difficult call when your connection fails and leaves a detailed summary so you can continue seamlessly. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for both protecting the customer experience and creating a strong handover, and recognize the continuity value of their support.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only for taking the call.$qb$, $qb$Say nothing because covering technical failures is normal teamwork.$qb$, $qb$Offer to take their next difficult call.$qb$, $qb$Thank the customer for being patient but not the teammate.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1282$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a proposal that is logically strong but assumes the reader already knows several internal abbreviations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the strong logic, identify abbreviations that could block the intended audience, and suggest defining or replacing them without changing the substance.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because internal readers should know the abbreviations.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to remove every abbreviation.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the proposal yourself in simpler language.$qb$, $qb$Say the proposal is unclear overall.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1283$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A program reports that adoption reached its target, but many users log in only once and never return. Leaders ask whether adoption is successful. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show initial adoption and repeat usage separately, explain what each indicates, and assess whether the program is creating sustained behavior rather than only first-time access.$qb$, $qb$Say adoption succeeded because the target was reached.$qb$, $qb$Say adoption failed because repeat usage is low.$qb$, $qb$Report only total logins.$qb$, $qb$Wait another year before discussing adoption quality.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1284$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone leaves a draft for your review with two possible recommendations and asks you to choose one. You need one local data point that will be available later today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the reasoning now, explain how the missing data would affect the choice, and leave a conditional recommendation or clear decision rule so the colleague can progress when the data arrives.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the data arrives before reviewing anything.$qb$, $qb$Choose the safer recommendation now.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to decide without the data.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting tomorrow instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1293$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A customer workshop is conducted in a language that one capable employee speaks well but not as their first language. A manager prefers a native speaker despite no evidence of communication problems. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess actual language proficiency and workshop requirements, and select on demonstrated ability rather than native-speaker status.$qb$, $qb$Choose the native speaker because customers may notice subtle differences.$qb$, $qb$Choose the non-native speaker automatically to demonstrate inclusion.$qb$, $qb$Ask the customer which accent they prefer.$qb$, $qb$Use only employees whose first language matches the customer.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1294$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You introduced a detailed weekly report to improve visibility. Stakeholders now say they read only two fields and the team spends hours preparing the rest. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the decisions the report supports, acknowledge the excess effort, and redesign it around the useful information rather than preserving your original format.$qb$, $qb$Keep the report because detailed visibility was the original objective.$qb$, $qb$Delete every field except the two stakeholders mention.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to prepare it faster.$qb$, $qb$Wait until stakeholders formally request a change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1296$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices that a customer-facing instruction is confusing and rewrites one sentence before you send it. The new wording is clearly better. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for improving the customer message, use the clearer wording, and recognize the value of constructive editing even though the original was yours.$qb$, $qb$Use the wording but do not mention the contribution.$qb$, $qb$Keep your wording because you own the communication.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to rewrite the entire instruction.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only if the customer later responds positively.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1328$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on an email that is polite and accurate but buries the requested action in the final paragraph. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the professional tone, point out that the action is hard to find, and suggest moving the request and deadline earlier while keeping necessary context concise.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the information is all there.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to make the email much shorter without explaining why.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the email yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the email is ineffective because it is too polite.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1329$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A change program reports that 90% of employees completed training, but error rates on the new process remain unchanged. Leaders ask whether the training was successful. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show completion as an activity measure, compare it with unchanged performance, investigate whether learning transferred to work, and avoid equating attendance with capability improvement.$qb$, $qb$Say training succeeded because completion exceeded target.$qb$, $qb$Say training failed because errors did not fall.$qb$, $qb$Report only the completion rate.$qb$, $qb$Wait for another training cycle before discussing effectiveness.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1330$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks for feedback on a draft. You can see one major issue immediately, while detailed review will take another hour you do not have today. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Flag the major issue now with enough explanation for them to act, state when the detailed review will follow, and avoid making them wait unnecessarily for all feedback.$qb$, $qb$Wait until you can provide the complete review.$qb$, $qb$Send only 'needs changes' and explain tomorrow.$qb$, $qb$Approve the draft except for minor details.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to delay their work until you have time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1339$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A development opportunity involves chairing meetings. A capable employee is quieter than usual candidates but has successfully facilitated small groups. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess facilitation evidence and assignment requirements, provide preparation if useful, and avoid equating a quiet style with inability to chair effectively.$qb$, $qb$Choose a more outspoken employee.$qb$, $qb$Give the quiet employee the role automatically.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to become more assertive first.$qb$, $qb$Limit them to note-taking until they speak more.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1340$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You designed a checklist that people now complete mechanically without noticing unusual cases. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the unintended behavior, identify which checks need judgment, and redesign the checklist to prompt attention to exceptions rather than simple box-ticking.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because completion rates are high.$qb$, $qb$Tell staff to pay more attention.$qb$, $qb$Remove the checklist entirely.$qb$, $qb$Add more boxes for unusual cases.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1342$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate spots a customer commitment in your draft that your evidence does not support. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for catching it, correct the commitment before sending, and improve how you verify promises in future drafts.$qb$, $qb$Remove it but do not discuss the review.$qb$, $qb$Defend it because the customer is likely to accept it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to own customer promises.$qb$, $qb$Keep it but soften the wording.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1374$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a technically correct document whose title and opening paragraph imply a stronger conclusion than the evidence supports.$qb$, $qb$Recognize the sound analysis, show how the framing overstates certainty, and align the title and opening with the actual evidence strength.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the detailed analysis is correct.$qb$, $qb$Change only the title to something neutral.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the document yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the whole document is unreliable.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1375$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project reports 100% delivery of planned features, but customers use only a small portion of them.$qb$, $qb$Present feature delivery and actual usage separately, examine which assumed needs did not translate into use, and judge success against realized value as well as output.$qb$, $qb$Call it successful because scope was delivered.$qb$, $qb$Call it unsuccessful because usage is low.$qb$, $qb$Report only usage.$qb$, $qb$Wait until next year to assess value.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1376$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks you to review a plan, but one section is outside your expertise and the relevant expert is offline.$qb$, $qb$Review the sections you can assess, clearly identify the expert-dependent section and question, and leave the plan moving rather than blocking everything.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the expert before reviewing anything.$qb$, $qb$Approve the whole plan except that section.$qb$, $qb$Guess based on your general experience.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting for tomorrow.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1385$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team chooses people for an overseas project partly on whether they have travelled internationally before. The work itself can be done remotely with one optional visit. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Separate genuine project requirements from historical travel experience and assess candidates on the skills needed, arranging the optional visit appropriately if it occurs.$qb$, $qb$Keep international experience as a selection criterion because it shows adaptability.$qb$, $qb$Select only employees who have never travelled to broaden opportunity.$qb$, $qb$Cancel the optional visit before choosing candidates.$qb$, $qb$Ask employees to disclose personal reasons they may not travel.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1386$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You proposed a daily check-in to improve coordination. After several weeks, the team says the same information is already visible in the tracker and few decisions occur in the call. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the original coordination need, acknowledge that the tracker now meets much of it, and reduce, redesign or remove the call while preserving a route for real blockers.$qb$, $qb$Keep the call because daily discipline is useful.$qb$, $qb$Cancel it immediately and provide no replacement for blockers.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to make the call shorter without changing its purpose.$qb$, $qb$Wait for your manager to suggest changing it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1388$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate spends time showing you a faster way to find an error rather than simply fixing it for you. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for teaching the method, apply it yourself to confirm understanding, and recognize that transferring capability is valuable beyond solving the immediate error.$qb$, $qb$Thank them for the fix even though they did not fix it themselves.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to use the method on your work whenever errors occur.$qb$, $qb$Say the explanation took longer than simply correcting the error.$qb$, $qb$Offer to teach them something in return before using the method.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1420$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a recommendation that is well argued but does not state what should happen next if the recommendation is accepted. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the strong reasoning, point out the missing action, owner or timing, and help make the recommendation executable without adding unnecessary detail.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because decision-makers can work out implementation.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to add a full project plan.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the next steps yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the recommendation is incomplete and provide no positive feedback.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1421$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project reports that customer complaints fell after launch, but support contacts through chat increased sharply. Leaders ask whether customer problems reduced. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Combine complaint and support-channel evidence, distinguish formal complaints from help-seeking behavior, and assess whether problems fell or simply shifted channel.$qb$, $qb$Say problems fell because complaints fell.$qb$, $qb$Say problems rose because chat contacts increased.$qb$, $qb$Add complaints and chats together without considering their meaning.$qb$, $qb$Report only the metric defined in the original project target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1422$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks whether they can proceed with a draft. You can approve the structure now, but one figure will be confirmed after they finish work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the structure conditionally, identify the exact figure that must be updated before release, and record the condition in the shared draft so work can continue safely.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the figure before giving any feedback.$qb$, $qb$Approve the full draft and assume the figure will be close.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to use last month's figure.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting for the next day.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1431$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A visible assignment is usually offered to people who can stay late at short notice, although this assignment has fixed daytime hours. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess candidates on the actual daytime assignment requirements and capability, not their ability to stay late when that is not required.$qb$, $qb$Choose the usual flexible employees.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the assignment for people who cannot work late.$qb$, $qb$Ask all candidates to commit to late work anyway.$qb$, $qb$Let the manager choose based on past availability.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1432$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You supported a new reporting metric that improves one team's score but encourages difficult cases to be delayed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the unintended incentive, measure delayed-case impact, and redesign or balance the metric around the intended outcome.$qb$, $qb$Keep the metric and warn people not to game it.$qb$, $qb$Remove the metric without replacing it.$qb$, $qb$Wait for a customer complaint.$qb$, $qb$Ask another team to own the measure.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1434$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate challenges your proposed schedule and shows that one dependency needs more time than you allowed. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank the teammate, revise the schedule using the dependency evidence, and improve how dependency durations are checked before commitment.$qb$, $qb$Keep your schedule to avoid appearing uncertain.$qb$, $qb$Add hidden buffer without explaining it.$qb$, $qb$Make the teammate responsible for any delay.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the dependency actually slips.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1466$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a clear report whose executive summary omits an important downside described later. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the report's clarity, show why the omitted downside is decision-relevant, and add it concisely to the executive summary.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the downside appears later.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to move every risk into the summary.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the summary yourself.$qb$, $qb$Reject the whole report as misleading.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1467$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project reports that training completion reached target, but users continue making the same process errors. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Present training completion and unchanged error performance separately, then investigate whether learning transferred to work before calling the program effective.$qb$, $qb$Say training succeeded because completion hit target.$qb$, $qb$Say training failed because errors did not fall.$qb$, $qb$Report only attendance.$qb$, $qb$Schedule more training immediately.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1468$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks for review; you can resolve most comments now, but one needs an expert who returns tomorrow. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Resolve all comments you can now, clearly mark the one expert-dependent point and its owner, and let the colleague continue with non-blocked work.$qb$, $qb$Wait until the expert returns before reviewing anything.$qb$, $qb$Guess the expert-dependent answer.$qb$, $qb$Send your partial review privately.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting tomorrow instead.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1477$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project role is usually given to employees who are comfortable speaking spontaneously. The actual work mostly involves careful written analysis and two prepared presentations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Select on analytical capability and prepared communication required by the role, rather than using spontaneous speaking style as the main indicator of suitability.$qb$, $qb$Choose the strongest spontaneous speaker because presentations are included.$qb$, $qb$Choose the quietest candidate to balance past selection.$qb$, $qb$Remove presentations from the role.$qb$, $qb$Ask candidates to give an unprepared speech as the main test.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1478$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You introduced a detailed approval matrix to reduce uncertainty. Teams now spend time escalating low-risk decisions that the matrix does not clearly cover. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the recurring low-risk cases, acknowledge the gap, and refine the matrix or principles so appropriate decisions can be made without unnecessary escalation.$qb$, $qb$Keep the matrix unchanged because people can always escalate.$qb$, $qb$Add every possible low-risk case to the matrix individually.$qb$, $qb$Tell teams to use judgment even if it conflicts with the matrix.$qb$, $qb$Remove the matrix entirely.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1480$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out that a shortcut you use creates extra work for another team. They also suggest an easy alternative. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for identifying both the impact and alternative, test the better approach, and recognize their contribution to improving the end-to-end process.$qb$, $qb$Use the alternative but do not discuss the criticism of your shortcut.$qb$, $qb$Keep your shortcut because it saves your own team time.$qb$, $qb$Ask the other team to adapt to the shortcut.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only if the alternative proves faster for your team too.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1512$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a customer proposal that is concise and persuasive but does not explain one important assumption behind the price. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the strong presentation, identify the assumption that could affect customer expectations, and add it clearly without overloading the proposal.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the price itself is correct.$qb$, $qb$Add every pricing assumption in full detail.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the proposal yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the proposal is misleading without explaining the specific gap.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1513$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A process improvement reports fewer manual steps, but employees now spend more time resolving exceptions created by the automation. Leaders ask whether efficiency improved. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show the reduction in routine steps together with exception workload and total effort, and judge efficiency on the end-to-end process rather than automation volume alone.$qb$, $qb$Say efficiency improved because manual steps fell.$qb$, $qb$Say automation failed because exceptions increased.$qb$, $qb$Report only the number of automated transactions.$qb$, $qb$Exclude exception work because it is not part of the normal process.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1514$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks for your approval before starting work. The plan is acceptable except for one minor point that can be corrected without changing the approach. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the approach, clearly request the minor correction in the shared record, and let work begin rather than blocking it for a non-material edit.$qb$, $qb$Withhold approval until every detail is corrected.$qb$, $qb$Approve without mentioning the issue.$qb$, $qb$Make the correction yourself without telling them.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting to discuss the minor point.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1523$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager prefers employees who are available for informal evening calls when selecting a daytime project team. Evening calls are not part of the project plan. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Select on the skills and availability actually required by the project and do not use informal evening access as a proxy for commitment.$qb$, $qb$Use evening availability because unexpected needs may arise.$qb$, $qb$Choose only employees who cannot attend evening calls to correct the imbalance.$qb$, $qb$Add evening availability to the formal project requirements.$qb$, $qb$Let the manager select whoever they trust most.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1524$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You introduced a mandatory pre-meeting template to improve preparation. People now spend more time filling it in than using it during meetings. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review which fields actually improve decisions, acknowledge the administrative burden, and simplify or remove the template where it does not add value.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because preparation discipline is important.$qb$, $qb$Tell people to complete the template faster.$qb$, $qb$Remove it immediately without checking which fields are useful.$qb$, $qb$Ask another team to redesign it for you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1526$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate catches an incorrect assumption in your analysis before you present it and explains how they spotted it. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for preventing the error, correct the analysis, and adopt the useful check they showed you for future work.$qb$, $qb$Correct the analysis but avoid discussing the mistake.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to review every future analysis.$qb$, $qb$Defend the assumption because the overall conclusion may still be right.$qb$, $qb$Thank them only if the final conclusion changes.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1558$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a proposal whose evidence is strong but whose recommendation uses absolute language such as 'will definitely succeed.' What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the strong evidence, show that the certainty exceeds what it proves, and calibrate the recommendation language to the actual confidence and risks.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because strong language is persuasive.$qb$, $qb$Replace 'definitely' with 'probably' and make no other review.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the proposal yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the evidence is weak because the wording is too certain.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1559$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A program reports fewer calls to support after a website redesign, but unsuccessful searches on the website have increased. Leaders ask whether customer effort fell. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show both support calls and failed searches, examine whether customers solved issues or simply stopped calling, and assess total customer effort across channels.$qb$, $qb$Say customer effort fell because calls decreased.$qb$, $qb$Say it rose because failed searches increased.$qb$, $qb$Add calls and searches together as one number.$qb$, $qb$Report only the support metric because that was the target.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1560$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks you to review a customer response. The core message is correct, but one attachment will be updated after they log off. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the message conditionally, identify the exact attachment that must be replaced before sending, and record the condition in the shared workspace.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the final attachment before reviewing anything.$qb$, $qb$Approve the whole package and assume the attachment will be changed.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to send without the attachment.$qb$, $qb$Schedule a meeting the next day.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1569$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A team tends to select employees who can attend informal weekend networking when choosing people for customer-facing opportunities. Weekend networking is not required for the work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Use job-relevant capability and actual work availability for selection, and provide equivalent relationship-building opportunities that do not depend on weekend attendance.$qb$, $qb$Keep using weekend attendance because it shows enthusiasm.$qb$, $qb$Stop all weekend networking events.$qb$, $qb$Reserve customer-facing work for people who never attend networking.$qb$, $qb$Ask candidates why they cannot attend weekends.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1570$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You introduced a detailed risk log, but the team now records many trivial items and struggles to see the material risks. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the intended purpose, define proportionate entry and escalation criteria, and simplify the log so material risks remain visible without hiding them in noise.$qb$, $qb$Keep every item because more risk information is safer.$qb$, $qb$Delete all low-rated risks immediately.$qb$, $qb$Ask the team to read the log more carefully.$qb$, $qb$Create a second risk log for important items.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1572$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate points out that your draft uses an outdated process name that customers may not recognize. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, update the wording to the current customer-facing name, and check nearby references so the correction is consistent throughout.$qb$, $qb$Change only the one phrase they noticed.$qb$, $qb$Keep the old name because long-term customers know it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the teammate to edit the whole document.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see whether customers are confused.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1604$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a presentation that is accurate and visually clear but gives the recommendation before explaining the decision criteria, making the choice seem arbitrary. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the clarity, show why the recommendation needs a visible link to the criteria, and restructure the story so the audience can follow the reasoning.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the recommendation is correct.$qb$, $qb$Move the recommendation to the final slide without explaining the criteria.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the presentation yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say the presentation is confusing without identifying why.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1605$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A new digital channel handles more customer requests, but total completion time is unchanged because many requests move to manual review later. Leaders ask whether digitization improved speed. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show digital uptake, manual-review rates and end-to-end completion time, and explain whether work was genuinely accelerated or only shifted between stages.$qb$, $qb$Say speed improved because more requests are digital.$qb$, $qb$Say digitization failed because total time is unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Report only digital processing time.$qb$, $qb$Exclude manual review because it happens after the digital step.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1606$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks for approval of a draft. One optional example is weak, but the core content is ready and their deadline arrives before you are online again. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the core content, clearly ask them to remove or improve the optional example before release, and avoid blocking the whole draft for a non-essential element.$qb$, $qb$Withhold approval until the example is fixed.$qb$, $qb$Approve everything without mentioning the weak example.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the example yourself later.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to delay the deadline.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1615$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A project lead prefers head-office employees for a high-profile assignment, although the needed expertise exists across locations. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess candidates on the expertise, collaboration and availability actually required, not head-office exposure unless it is genuinely relevant.$qb$, $qb$Choose head-office staff because they know senior leaders.$qb$, $qb$Reserve the assignment for remote employees to balance access.$qb$, $qb$Require remote candidates to spend time at head office first.$qb$, $qb$Let the lead choose familiar people.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1616$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You introduced a detailed handover form. Teams now copy large amounts of text into it, but key decisions remain hard to find. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Redesign the form around decisions, risks and next actions, removing fields that add volume without helping the receiver.$qb$, $qb$Keep it and tell teams to write more clearly.$qb$, $qb$Add a summary box but keep every field.$qb$, $qb$Remove the form without replacing the useful parts.$qb$, $qb$Ask receivers to search the handover.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1618$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices your meeting pack uses an old customer logo and sends the current one before distribution. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, replace it, and check nearby customer references so the pack is consistently current.$qb$, $qb$Replace only the logo without acknowledging the help.$qb$, $qb$Keep the old logo because content matters more.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to update the whole pack.$qb$, $qb$Wait to see if the customer notices.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1650$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for comments on a proposal with strong analysis, but the opening claim sounds more certain than the supporting evidence allows. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the clarity and restructure the story so criteria and evidence visibly lead to the recommendation.$qb$, $qb$Approve it because the recommendation is correct.$qb$, $qb$Move the recommendation to the final slide only.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite it yourself.$qb$, $qb$Say it is confusing without explaining why.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1651$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A digital intake channel now handles most requests, yet many of those requests are later moved into manual review and overall completion time has barely changed. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$Show digital uptake, manual-review rates and end-to-end completion time, and judge whether work was accelerated or merely shifted.$qb$, $qb$Say speed improved because more requests are digital.$qb$, $qb$Say digitization failed because total time is unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Report only digital processing time.$qb$, $qb$Exclude manual review.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-1652$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague working several hours ahead has prepared a document for release. One optional illustration needs improvement, but the main content is ready and their deadline arrives before your next overlap. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the core content and clearly require the optional example to be removed or improved before release, rather than blocking the whole draft.$qb$, $qb$Withhold approval until the example is fixed.$qb$, $qb$Approve everything without mentioning it.$qb$, $qb$Rewrite the example later yourself.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to delay the deadline.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2158$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone can proceed; one minor comment remains but does not affect approach or release criteria. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the approach, record the non-blocking comment, and let work proceed rather than delaying for minor refinement.$qb$, $qb$Send a high-level summary message and offer to clarify details during a live call later.$qb$, $qb$Send multiple short messages as new details arise throughout the workday.$qb$, $qb$Wait until both parties are online simultaneously before sharing critical project updates.$qb$, $qb$Forward raw file attachments without context or clear action requests.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2351$qb$, $qb$A4-03$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A stretch assignment needs predictable travel two days a month. A strong employee with caregiving responsibilities asks for advance scheduling. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Assess whether predictable travel meets the role need and judge capability and actual availability rather than assumptions about caregiving.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2354$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate stays late to correct a data issue you introduced and prevents a customer delay. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them specifically, acknowledge your part in creating the issue, and fix the process weakness that caused it.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2386$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A report has strong evidence, but the recommendation is buried after several pages of background. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Recognize the evidence quality and help move the recommendation and decision logic forward while retaining necessary support.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2398$qb$, $qb$A4-01$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You introduced a weekly completion target. Staff now close simple cases quickly while leaving older complex cases untouched. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Balance the target with ageing, complexity and customer impact measures so the metric rewards meaningful progress rather than easy-case selection.$qb$, $qb$Respond to a weekly completion target staff now with the standard explanation before clarifying the other person's actual need.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail about a weekly completion target staff now even though the problem is relevance or clarity rather than missing information.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to take over a weekly completion target staff now without preserving the context already available.$qb$, $qb$Treat the concern about a weekly completion target staff now as resistance and continue with the original message.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2400$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate spots that you have used their analysis in a senior update without mentioning their contribution. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Acknowledge the omission, correct the attribution where practical, thank them specifically, and improve how contributions are captured before future updates.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2432$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a presentation with a strong recommendation but too much evidence on each slide. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Preserve the strong recommendation, help select the few pieces of evidence that drive the decision, and move supporting detail to backup material.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2434$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone sends three questions. You can answer two now; the third depends on tomorrow's legal advice. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Answer the two independent questions now, clearly mark the third as pending legal advice, and state when they should expect that answer.$qb$, $qb$Respond to in another time zone sends three with the standard explanation before clarifying the other person's actual need.$qb$, $qb$Add more detail about in another time zone sends three even though the problem is relevance or clarity rather than missing information.$qb$, $qb$Ask another colleague to take over in another time zone sends three without preserving the context already available.$qb$, $qb$Treat the concern about in another time zone sends three as resistance and continue with the original message.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2538$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices that a customer-facing document contains a chart they created but their name was removed during editing. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them for raising it, correct the attribution where appropriate, and check whether the editing process is stripping contributor credits elsewhere.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2570$qb$, $qb$A4-07$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks for feedback on a report whose recommendation is clear but whose evidence is spread across several sections. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the clear recommendation and consolidate the decision-relevant evidence so readers can see why the conclusion follows.$qb$, $qb$Implement a practical interim adjustment to address immediate operational needs while monitoring results.$qb$, $qb$Maintain existing procedures temporarily until formal company-wide guidance is published.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the issue to departmental leadership without first gathering supporting data or options.$qb$, $qb$Ignore the operational discrepancy and proceed with business as usual.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2572$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks whether to start. All core requirements are agreed, but one optional visual style choice is unresolved. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm they can begin the core work, mark the visual choice as non-blocking, and resolve it asynchronously.$qb$, $qb$Send a high-level summary message and offer to clarify details during a live call later.$qb$, $qb$Send multiple short messages as new details arise throughout the workday.$qb$, $qb$Wait until both parties are online simultaneously before sharing critical project updates.$qb$, $qb$Forward raw file attachments without context or clear action requests.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2584$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices that your senior update omits a colleague who supplied the analysis behind a key recommendation. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Correct the attribution where appropriate, thank the teammate for catching it, and improve how contributors are recorded before future updates.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2618$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks for approval. The main deliverable is ready, but one optional reference link has not been checked. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Approve the substantive deliverable, mark the optional link as non-blocking or remove it until checked, and let work continue.$qb$, $qb$Send a high-level summary message and offer to clarify details during a live call later.$qb$, $qb$Send multiple short messages as new details arise throughout the workday.$qb$, $qb$Wait until both parties are online simultaneously before sharing critical project updates.$qb$, $qb$Forward raw file attachments without context or clear action requests.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2630$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate catches that a senior presentation uses a graph they built, but the slide credits only the presenter. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, correct the contribution credit where appropriate, and improve how source and contributor attribution is retained during editing.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2664$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks if they should proceed. The main scope is agreed, but one optional example still needs customer permission. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm they can proceed with the agreed scope, keep the optional example out until permission is obtained, and avoid blocking independent work.$qb$, $qb$Send a high-level summary message and offer to clarify details during a live call later.$qb$, $qb$Send multiple short messages as new details arise throughout the workday.$qb$, $qb$Wait until both parties are online simultaneously before sharing critical project updates.$qb$, $qb$Forward raw file attachments without context or clear action requests.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2756$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague can proceed with core analysis, but one optional quote still needs source verification. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm they can proceed with the core analysis and hold or remove the unverified quote.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2768$qb$, $qb$A4-04$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate notices that your executive summary uses their risk analysis but removes the caveat that made the conclusion conditional. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Thank them, restore the material caveat, check the summary against the underlying analysis, and preserve accurate credit for their contribution.$qb$, $qb$Address the immediate operational requirement with a localized fix while monitoring progress.$qb$, $qb$Maintain the current approach and wait for other departments to adapt their workflows.$qb$, $qb$Escalate the situation to department heads without proposing potential solutions.$qb$, $qb$Disengage from the issue and let other stakeholders resolve the friction independently.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-2802$qb$, $qb$A4-05$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague in another time zone asks to start drafting. The required sections are agreed, but one optional case study has not been approved for use. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Confirm they can draft the required sections, exclude the case study until approved, and avoid delaying independent work.$qb$, $qb$Send a high-level summary message and offer to clarify details during a live call later.$qb$, $qb$Send multiple short messages as new details arise throughout the workday.$qb$, $qb$Wait until both parties are online simultaneously before sharing critical project updates.$qb$, $qb$Forward raw file attachments without context or clear action requests.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-055$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$During an internal review, you notice that a control failure was omitted from a summary because it was corrected before any loss occurred. Including it may attract extra scrutiny. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ensure the failure is recorded accurately with its impact, correction, and preventive action so the review is complete and not misleading.$qb$, $qb$Ask the control owner whether they want the issue included in the summary.$qb$, $qb$Mention the issue verbally during the meeting but leave the written summary unchanged.$qb$, $qb$Leave it out because no loss occurred and the control has already been fixed.$qb$, $qb$Remove other minor issues as well so the summary focuses only on material losses.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-101$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager suggests recording a delayed action as completed on time because the delay had no customer impact and the metric affects a bonus. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the actual completion date, explain why the record must remain accurate, and address the delay through the proper performance process.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to confirm the instruction in writing before changing the date.$qb$, $qb$Change the date but add a private note about what actually happened.$qb$, $qb$Leave the field blank so the metric is not affected.$qb$, $qb$Change the date because no customer was harmed.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-135$qb$, $qb$A4-06$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A cost-saving initiative reduced spending but also increased processing time. Leaders ask whether it should continue. What do you present?$qb$, $qb$A decision-focused story showing the savings, time impact, affected stakeholders, trade-off, and your evidence-based recommendation.$qb$, $qb$A detailed table of all monthly cost and time numbers.$qb$, $qb$Only the savings because that was the initiative's objective.$qb$, $qb$Only the processing delay because it affects operations.$qb$, $qb$A neutral summary with no recommendation.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-145$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to backdate a routine approval because the underlying work was completed correctly but the approval step was forgotten. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Record the approval at the actual time, document that the control step was missed, and follow the required correction or exception process.$qb$, $qb$Record the approval today and add a note that the work itself was completed earlier.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague's manager whether backdating is acceptable in this case before making the entry.$qb$, $qb$Backdate the approval because the underlying work was correct and no customer was affected.$qb$, $qb$Let the colleague use your credentials to enter the earlier date so you are not the person making the change.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-191$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A friend in another department asks you to send a restricted internal report because it would help them prepare for a meeting. They are not on the access list. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not send it; explain that access is restricted and direct them to the report owner or formal access process.$qb$, $qb$Ask the report owner whether your friend can receive it and wait for approval.$qb$, $qb$Send only the pages you believe are relevant to their meeting.$qb$, $qb$Let your friend view the report on your screen without sending a copy.$qb$, $qb$Send it because they are an employee and have a legitimate business reason.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-237$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a colleague has copied a real customer's personal details into a training example that will be used with new employees. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop the material from being used, replace the real details with approved fictional or masked data, and follow the required privacy process for the inappropriate use already made.$qb$, $qb$Remove the customer's name but leave the other details because trainees need a realistic example.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to replace the example before the next training session.$qb$, $qb$Use the material only with employees who already have access to customer systems.$qb$, $qb$Keep it because the example is internal and intended for learning.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-283$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You are asked to complete a compliance checklist for work performed by a colleague who is unavailable. You can see the final output but cannot verify two required steps. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Complete only what you can verify, mark the two steps as unconfirmed, and follow the required process to obtain evidence or escalate the incomplete check.$qb$, $qb$Mark the steps complete because the final output appears correct.$qb$, $qb$Leave the entire checklist blank until the colleague returns.$qb$, $qb$Ask another teammate whether the colleague normally performs those steps and use their answer.$qb$, $qb$Copy the previous checklist because the work process is usually the same.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-329$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You discover that a colleague has been using another employee's system login because their own access request is delayed. The work itself is legitimate. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Ask them to stop using shared credentials, arrange the proper access or approved temporary route, and report or document the credential-sharing issue as required.$qb$, $qb$Tell them to stop sharing the login once their own access is approved.$qb$, $qb$Allow it for urgent work but ask them not to access anything outside their role.$qb$, $qb$Ask the account owner to supervise the colleague whenever the login is used.$qb$, $qb$Ignore it because both employees are authorized to perform the same work.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-375$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You accidentally see performance information about a colleague that you were not intended to receive. The information is personally interesting but irrelevant to your work. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Stop reviewing it, notify the sender or appropriate owner, and follow the required instruction for deleting or securing the information.$qb$, $qb$Close the file and delete it without telling anyone.$qb$, $qb$Keep the file unopened in case someone later asks what was sent.$qb$, $qb$Tell a trusted colleague that you received sensitive information by mistake and ask what they would do.$qb$, $qb$Read enough to understand whether the information affects your team before deleting it.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-421$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to approve an expense because their manager is unavailable. You are not an authorized approver, but the expense appears legitimate and urgent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Explain that you cannot approve it, help identify the authorized backup or exception route, and keep the urgency visible without bypassing the control.$qb$, $qb$Approve it this once because the expense appears legitimate.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to wait until their manager returns.$qb$, $qb$Tell the colleague to submit it under a different category that does not require the same approval.$qb$, $qb$Ask another unauthorized colleague to approve it so the delay is shared.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-467$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to share a confidential customer list through a personal messaging app because the approved system is temporarily unavailable. The work is urgent. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not use the personal app; use an approved contingency channel or escalate the system issue and urgency through the required route.$qb$, $qb$Send only the customer names through the personal app and keep other details out.$qb$, $qb$Send the list if the colleague promises to delete it immediately.$qb$, $qb$Wait silently for the approved system to return, even if the urgent work is missed.$qb$, $qb$Use the personal app because business continuity is more important during an outage.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-513$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to confirm that a mandatory check was completed yesterday. You remember discussing it, but there is no record and you cannot remember whether the actual check occurred. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$State that you cannot verify completion, seek the required evidence or repeat the check if appropriate, and record the true status rather than relying on memory.$qb$, $qb$Confirm it because the discussion strongly suggests the check was done.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague whether they remember completing it and use their answer.$qb$, $qb$Mark it complete but add a note that documentation is missing.$qb$, $qb$Leave the field blank and say nothing unless someone asks.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-559$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks you to remove an unfavorable comment from a customer summary because it is 'not representative' and may distract senior leaders. The comment is genuine and relevant to a known issue. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Keep the relevant comment or accurately summarize the issue, provide context about representativeness, and avoid removing material evidence solely because it is unfavorable.$qb$, $qb$Remove the comment but mention generally that a few customers raised concerns.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager to decide because they own the senior presentation.$qb$, $qb$Keep the comment only in an appendix where it is less prominent.$qb$, $qb$Remove it because one unrepresentative comment could mislead leaders.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-605$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to enter a system approval on their behalf because they completed the review but cannot access the system before the deadline. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not record their approval as your own; use the authorized backup or exception process and preserve who actually reviewed and approved the work.$qb$, $qb$Enter the approval and note in comments that the colleague performed the review.$qb$, $qb$Enter it because you trust the colleague and the deadline is real.$qb$, $qb$Ask for their password so the approval appears under the correct name.$qb$, $qb$Let the deadline pass without trying to find an authorized alternative.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-651$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$You notice that a report due today includes a figure copied from an unverified draft source. The figure is plausible and replacing it will delay submission. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Verify the figure or clearly remove or qualify it before submission, and communicate any necessary delay rather than presenting unverified data as confirmed.$qb$, $qb$Submit the report and verify the figure afterward because it is probably correct.$qb$, $qb$Keep the figure but mark it with an asterisk saying it may change.$qb$, $qb$Ask your manager whether the deadline is more important than verification.$qb$, $qb$Replace it with your best estimate so the report can go on time.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-697$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A teammate suggests changing the date on a document so it appears a required review happened before a deadline. The review was completed correctly, but one day late. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Refuse to alter the record, keep the true review date, and report or manage the late completion through the appropriate process.$qb$, $qb$Use the deadline date because the review itself was completed correctly.$qb$, $qb$Leave the date blank so the lateness is not highlighted.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager whether the one-day difference is material.$qb$, $qb$Change the date if no customer or financial impact resulted.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-743$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague offers to send you customer data from their personal email because the company mail service is temporarily slow. You need the data for an urgent analysis. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Decline the personal-email transfer and use an approved secure alternative or escalate the service issue so the urgent analysis can proceed without bypassing data controls.$qb$, $qb$Accept the file if it is password protected.$qb$, $qb$Ask them to remove names before sending it personally.$qb$, $qb$Wait for company email even if the analysis misses its deadline.$qb$, $qb$Accept it this once and delete the personal email afterward.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-789$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A manager asks you to copy their approval from last month's similar case into today's record because they are travelling and cannot access the system. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Do not reuse the old approval; arrange an authorized current approval or documented exception that reflects today's case and actual approver.$qb$, $qb$Copy it because the cases are materially the same.$qb$, $qb$Use the old approval but note today's date beside it.$qb$, $qb$Wait for the manager to return even if the case becomes late.$qb$, $qb$Ask another employee to paste the manager's approval for you.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-835$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to sign a completion record for work you did not observe because they say all evidence is in the file. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Review the evidence required for your sign-off and sign only if your role permits evidence-based verification; otherwise direct the record to the appropriate verifier.$qb$, $qb$Sign because the evidence is available if anyone later checks it.$qb$, $qb$Ask the colleague to assure you verbally that the work was completed.$qb$, $qb$Refuse every sign-off unless you personally observed the work.$qb$, $qb$Sign but add a note that you relied on the colleague's statement.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
  ($qb$ITEM-SJT-881$qb$, $qb$A4-02$qb$, $qb$SJT$qb$, $qb$A colleague asks you to omit a known control exception because it was corrected before any harm occurred. What do you do?$qb$, $qb$Report the exception as required, include the correction and impact context, and do not erase a required event merely because it was recovered.$qb$, $qb$Leave it out because the control ultimately worked.$qb$, $qb$Mention it verbally but omit it from the report.$qb$, $qb$Ask the manager whether it is important enough to include.$qb$, $qb$Include it only if it happens again.$qb$, 5, 3, 2, 1, 0, true),
